1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
You don't have to learn everything, actually,

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,440
as far as you know.

3
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:05,040
So remember, big strategy.

4
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:07,040
If you just learn everything, then you're safe.

5
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,280
So just learn it all.

6
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:09,780
All right.

7
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:12,400
So last thing we were talking about in the last class

8
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:13,680
was nominal rigidity.

9
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,840
And there is a classic solution to nominal rigidity,

10
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,640
and that is just to inflate it away.

11
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:30,720
So if you've got real wages stuck at 10%

12
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:33,720
above the market clarity level due to nominal rigidity,

13
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:36,040
you could either try to somehow wait around

14
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:38,360
for the wages to go down, or you could just

15
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,920
go and pump up the money supply by 10%,

16
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:44,200
wait for prices to rise by 10%, and then the real wages

17
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:47,680
will actually be fine just the way they are.

18
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:48,720
All right.

19
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:50,680
Now, practice is more easily said than done.

20
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:54,240
So there's a really neat paper from Robert Lucas

21
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:57,480
in the 70s showing that the countries where inflation works,

22
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:59,280
the countries with very little inflation.

23
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:01,000
And the countries where it doesn't work

24
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,840
are the countries with a lot of inflation.

25
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:04,040
Now, if you wonder, how do you get

26
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:05,840
to be one of these countries with lots of inflation that

27
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,880
doesn't work, the answer is you start off with low inflation,

28
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,320
and then you try it, and it works.

29
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:12,320
And then you try it again, and it works worse.

30
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:14,320
And you keep going and doing more in order

31
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:15,960
to try to get over it.

32
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,920
So it's sometimes been compared to being a drug addict, where

33
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:23,360
you keep increasing the dose because you need more and more

34
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:25,200
in order to get the same hit.

35
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:26,600
Of course, what's really going on

36
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:30,800
is that people get used to the idea of thinking in real terms

37
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:32,200
when inflation is high enough.

38
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,280
So if you've ever been in a high inflation country,

39
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:36,200
you may notice that people right on the street

40
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:38,360
seem to do real nominal calculations very easily

41
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:39,280
in their heads.

42
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:42,320
More or less in Turkey, the kids could go and do

43
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:43,600
five different from the others.

44
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:47,520
This was actually like 1997 when inflation was higher in Turkey.

45
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:49,840
But kids on the street could do calculations

46
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:52,600
in five different exchange rates very easily.

47
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:55,360
And they would tell you that if there was any disparity,

48
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:56,760
they would know which of the five

49
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:59,560
was actually the best deal.

50
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,120
So places where inflation is high,

51
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,520
people get very used to thinking in real terms.

52
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,680
Places where inflation is low, that's

53
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:08,520
where people are likely to make mistakes.

54
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:10,440
I know that when I give undergraduates

55
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:13,760
real versus nominal questions, they generally just

56
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,600
mess them up very badly.

57
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,640
So of course, maybe the kids would also

58
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:20,440
mess it up in front of a paper and pencil,

59
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,000
but on the street, they do it well.

60
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,880
There's actually research in psychology

61
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:27,000
on how kids who are street vendors

62
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,640
can do complicated math that they can't do in a classroom,

63
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:33,920
as long as you talk about selling papayas,

64
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:34,760
something like that.

65
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:36,960
So something that they're used to selling and buying

66
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:39,160
that the math actually works.

67
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:40,440
How could that be?

68
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,440
That's how actual thinking is.

69
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:43,720
It's not logic.

70
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:47,200
It's psycho logic.

71
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:51,640
So questions about inflating weight and homogeneities?

72
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:52,160
Pretty basic.

73
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:52,920
All right.

74
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:57,320
And then, last topic on labor, efficiency wages.

75
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,520
A very unfortunately named topic,

76
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:10,080
because it suggests that these are the only efficient wages,

77
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:11,920
or these are the efficient wages.

78
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:15,880
And not only are there other models where the wage is also

79
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:17,880
efficient, but in efficiency wage models,

80
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:19,760
the wage is not efficient, as it turns out.

81
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:23,360
So I cannot help the standard language,

82
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:25,440
so you just have to get used to it.

83
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:27,040
So I'll just put it in quote marks.

84
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:30,000
Quote efficiency wages is what we're talking about.

85
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:30,560
All right.

86
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,920
Now, just for background.

87
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:35,840
When you've got unimplicit working conditions

88
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,880
in a standard supply and demand model for labor,

89
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:42,640
so we've got L, W, we've got supply, we've got demand.

90
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:44,720
So this is for a normal occupation.

91
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:48,960
And suppose that something changes about the occupation

92
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:51,680
so that it becomes an unpleasant job to do.

93
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,160
Which curve shifts, first of all?

94
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:55,160
Supply, yes.

95
00:03:55,160 --> 00:03:57,120
Because does the fact that you're miserable

96
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:00,520
mean that your employers want to pay you more in and of itself?

97
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:02,640
Yeah, like if you're going through personal trauma,

98
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:04,400
does your boss say, well, market wages now

99
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:07,120
require me to give you a raise because you've got

100
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:10,560
problems, your son's a disappointment.

101
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:14,160
That's not how it works.

102
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:16,760
So demand does not shift at all just

103
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:19,120
because the job is pleasant or unpleasant.

104
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:20,440
Instead, what shifts is supply.

105
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:22,160
People don't want to do it.

106
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:24,160
And of course, when the job is unpleasant,

107
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:26,440
supply shifts which way?

108
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:27,840
Yes, yes.

109
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:30,240
Supply falls.

110
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:33,280
And notice, this does have the amazing effect

111
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:34,680
of raising wages.

112
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,440
So it's not that people are totally

113
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:39,520
confused when they think that somehow the boss has

114
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,400
to pay you more for the unpleasant job.

115
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:45,080
It's just that it doesn't come out of any need

116
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:47,200
to make you feel better right away.

117
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:49,480
It doesn't because they want you more.

118
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:51,360
It's because there's fewer of you around,

119
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:53,320
and so you're more valuable.

120
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:56,960
So this is the very basic stuff here.

121
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:58,840
And when this occurs, this difference

122
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:03,320
between the regular wage and the wage in the unpleasant job,

123
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:06,240
it's got a special name called a compensating differential.

124
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:11,240
So it's a differential in the wage

125
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:16,800
that compensates people for having an unpleasant job.

126
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:19,040
Now, suppose there's a job where you're just

127
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:22,400
required to work really fast all the time.

128
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:24,200
Is that any different from a job that's

129
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:28,440
smelly or disgusting or socially frowned upon?

130
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:30,840
Or is there any difference between a job

131
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:33,520
where you just have to work double time all the time?

132
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:34,020
Not really.

133
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:35,920
No, it's exactly the same thing.

134
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:38,320
All right, a job where I have to be running all the time,

135
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:39,400
great.

136
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:41,360
Or a job where it smells bad all the time,

137
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:43,360
or a job where the customers spit on me.

138
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:46,120
All of these are the same kind of thing.

139
00:05:46,120 --> 00:05:49,800
All of these very naturally go and reduce supply,

140
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:52,240
which then raises wages.

141
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:54,880
Very simple.

142
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:57,640
All right, so as long as we got symmetric information,

143
00:05:57,640 --> 00:06:01,760
as long as both sides know what's going on,

144
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:04,120
then you can get workers to work harder just

145
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:05,800
by paying them more wages.

146
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:07,080
So you're right, fine.

147
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:09,120
You have to go and do something unpleasant.

148
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:14,320
Well, then supply will be lower, and then wages will be higher.

149
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:17,360
But this is a symmetric information model.

150
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:23,480
This is a model where both sides know what's going on.

151
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:29,040
But what if we've got asymmetric information where

152
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:30,960
the workers know more about their effort

153
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:32,680
than the employers?

154
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:35,400
So the workers know if they're working double time,

155
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:37,960
and the employers just have to guess.

156
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:40,440
Is he really working double time or not?

157
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:44,480
The employer, of course, knows how fast he's working.

158
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:48,600
The employer, on the other hand, has to wonder.

159
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:50,640
Now, you may remember there's the question, well,

160
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:52,400
why can't you just look at the output?

161
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:53,880
Why can't you just see, well, he's

162
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:55,000
really working double time.

163
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,680
How could he only produce five widgets?

164
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:00,120
And if you may remember, the standard answer

165
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:02,480
is, in that case, the asymmetric information problem

166
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:04,320
just solves itself, and we're basically

167
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:06,280
back in the symmetric information world.

168
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:09,880
But suppose that there are other reasons.

169
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:13,240
There are valid excuses for why you would only produce five.

170
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:16,600
Like maybe some customers are really difficult,

171
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:19,080
and they sit there while you're supposed

172
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:20,960
to be running the cashier.

173
00:07:20,960 --> 00:07:22,520
But maybe you get unlucky, and you

174
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:26,080
get the really slow customers who sit there going, no.

175
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:27,080
I don't know.

176
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,360
On the one hand, I could get a hamburger.

177
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:34,240
But all right, if you get a bunch of customers like that,

178
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:35,860
you will only ring up a few customers,

179
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,280
even though you were doing exactly what you're supposed to.

180
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:40,760
And if employers know that might happen,

181
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:44,320
but they don't know whether it did in fact happen,

182
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:48,440
then again, we're back in this asymmetric information world.

183
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:51,920
Now, a standard solution to this asymmetric information

184
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:57,040
is to say, look, maybe you'll go and cheat,

185
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:59,360
and you'll shirk on the job, and you won't work fast

186
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:01,280
like you're supposed to.

187
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:05,520
And maybe you'll get away with it, but maybe you won't.

188
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:10,920
And if I ever catch you going and slacking on my dime,

189
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:12,960
you're fired.

190
00:08:12,960 --> 00:08:15,320
This sounds like a pretty good tactic.

191
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:17,780
But there's a little weird thing about competitive markets.

192
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:19,760
So if you've really got perfect competition,

193
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:23,200
and you lose your job, what can you immediately do

194
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:24,880
with no transaction cost at all?

195
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:25,600
Get another job.

196
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:27,680
Get another job that is just as good.

197
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:29,000
So how scary is that threat?

198
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:30,420
Do you know what's going to happen

199
00:08:30,420 --> 00:08:32,500
if you don't go and do just what I say, something

200
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:33,480
very unpleasant?

201
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,940
You're going to have to go and find another job at no cost

202
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:38,040
yourself, and you'll be just as good as this one.

203
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:41,160
Wow, that's really scary there.

204
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:42,840
That's absolutely terrible that I

205
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:47,400
would have to endure that kind of humiliation.

206
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:50,200
So the idea of efficiency wages comes from saying, well,

207
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:53,560
then what could you do to actually motivate

208
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:54,720
your workers?

209
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:57,560
What can you do to make the threat to fire them scary?

210
00:08:57,560 --> 00:08:59,220
Well, of course, one thing you could do

211
00:08:59,220 --> 00:09:02,520
is to make sure that their next job is terrible.

212
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:04,480
But that's hard to do, right?

213
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:06,040
So yeah, there is the old, you'll

214
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:08,600
never work in this town again after what you've done.

215
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:10,800
But I've never seen a movie that actually bothered

216
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:13,440
to follow through on the threat and show the person

217
00:09:13,440 --> 00:09:14,600
unable to get another job.

218
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:16,560
It's more of just an empty threat, right?

219
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:17,280
But there we go.

220
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:19,520
But what, you control the entire industry?

221
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:20,960
Everyone in the industry likes you?

222
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:22,440
That sounds like a real thing.

223
00:09:22,440 --> 00:09:25,240
Like, how long do people even remember what I did to you?

224
00:09:25,240 --> 00:09:27,360
You may remember how I betrayed you for years,

225
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:30,440
but no one else cares.

226
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:34,320
All right, so since you can't go and effectively ruin

227
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:37,040
people's options with other employers,

228
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:39,400
at least that doesn't seem too likely.

229
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:42,160
So if I can't get you to appreciate your job

230
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:44,440
by ruining your other options, what

231
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:47,040
is the other route to make you appreciate your job?

232
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:48,240
Make the job you have better.

233
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,480
Yes, make the job that you have better so that you

234
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:53,360
would be scared to lose it.

235
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:55,240
This is how people control you.

236
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:57,200
They give you a good deal and then

237
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:00,640
threaten to take it away if you disappoint them.

238
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:01,140
Right?

239
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:03,240
Really, whatever you're worried about losing,

240
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:05,280
that's because you're getting a good deal, right?

241
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:07,240
If you're just getting a totally mediocre deal,

242
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:11,480
then why would the threat to lose it be bad?

243
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:13,640
All right, so anyway, and then, of course,

244
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:16,520
if we've got this is our market clearing wage,

245
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:18,240
or this is a market clearing wage,

246
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:22,000
what we need to do is to move the wage above that.

247
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:23,760
So you move the wage above that.

248
00:10:23,760 --> 00:10:26,200
And when you move the wage above the market clearing wage,

249
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:30,400
we get which of two market outcomes, both of which

250
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:31,400
start with S?

251
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:31,900
Surplus.

252
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:32,400
Surplus.

253
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:34,240
Yes, you get a labor surplus.

254
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:36,280
And in English, what do we call a labor surplus?

255
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:36,840
Unemployment.

256
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:37,560
Unemployment.

257
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:41,640
Yes, a labor surplus and unemployment are the same thing.

258
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:44,440
They are the same thing.

259
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:47,480
And notice, once you're getting this high wage,

260
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:49,240
then you're worried about losing your job.

261
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:52,160
And then if someone threatens to fire you, that is bad.

262
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:52,660
All right?

263
00:10:52,660 --> 00:10:53,160
Right?

264
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:54,920
Because then it's going to be hard for you

265
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:57,680
to find another job that is just as good as your last job.

266
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:01,440
That is the whole idea of United Nations wages.

267
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:03,960
Now, it's natural for people to say, well, that doesn't really

268
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:05,440
explain anything.

269
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:08,960
Because if everyone offers above market wages,

270
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:13,040
then it's no longer scary to lose your job.

271
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:15,840
But actually, if you look more closely, it's still scary.

272
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:19,800
Because suppose that all employers are there.

273
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:21,880
What is the bad thing that can happen to you

274
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:22,880
if you lose your job?

275
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:23,880
Can't find another.

276
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:25,920
You just can't find another job at all.

277
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:29,480
So yeah, so the problem is, even if all jobs that exist

278
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:32,560
pay high wages above the market-faring level,

279
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:35,040
the problem is that you may be unable to find

280
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:35,880
another job at all.

281
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:38,440
So either you'll find another job that's as good,

282
00:11:38,440 --> 00:11:39,440
then everything's fine.

283
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:41,440
Or you'll be stuck, and you won't be able to find

284
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:44,080
another job.

285
00:11:44,080 --> 00:11:46,360
And if you're thinking, you're wondering

286
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:49,280
about an analogy that may be relevant to your future,

287
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:52,400
a very standard outcome when people have a 10-year track job

288
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:55,960
is they are desperate to get tenure.

289
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:58,320
Now, why are people so desperate to get tenure?

290
00:12:02,040 --> 00:12:04,320
Why are people so desperate for tenure again?

291
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:06,000
I can never remember.

292
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:14,280
Because that job is better than the next job

293
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:15,920
they're likely to have.

294
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:20,920
So I've actually been on the Tenure Promotion and Oversight

295
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:25,160
Committee for the College of Arts and Sciences.

296
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:27,440
So I can definitely say that there

297
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:30,440
are people that receive strong votes to get tenure

298
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:32,880
in certain departments, which will remain in the main list,

299
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:36,160
which might be English.

300
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:38,640
And you look at the BDA, and you're like,

301
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:41,080
I don't think they could get a job at a community college

302
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:41,680
with this BDA.

303
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:44,200
They didn't even publish the dissertation.

304
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:46,760
They had 15 years to do it.

305
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:50,440
And yet, they got maybe 100% vote for tenure.

306
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:52,840
And they're desperate to get it because they

307
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:55,200
know that if they don't get tenure,

308
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:57,240
then they are going to be struggling to get a job

309
00:12:57,240 --> 00:12:58,920
at a community college, likely.

310
00:12:58,920 --> 00:13:00,620
And on the other hand, they could easily

311
00:13:00,620 --> 00:13:03,000
be replaced with lots of people at community colleges

312
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,360
who publish far better.

313
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:06,920
There are people at community colleges

314
00:13:06,920 --> 00:13:08,320
who publish far better than people

315
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:10,120
who get a 10-year track jobs.

316
00:13:10,120 --> 00:13:13,160
But there is a giant system of corrupt nepotism

317
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:16,880
that exists in universities below the top level.

318
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:19,120
So this is all good information for you, by the way.

319
00:13:19,120 --> 00:13:23,160
Write it down, giant system of corrupt nepotism.

320
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:24,440
Yes.

321
00:13:24,440 --> 00:13:27,800
So out of all of the key choke points

322
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:30,600
in preventing you from getting a dream job for life, which

323
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:34,800
is being a tenured professor, the really key one

324
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:38,680
is just getting that first tenure track job.

325
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:40,600
Those are genuinely hard to get.

326
00:13:40,600 --> 00:13:42,320
Not so much economics, I'll leave it there.

327
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:43,280
Not easy.

328
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:45,200
But anyway, once you get that, then actually,

329
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:47,480
if you were at a school ranked below for a worse than 50

330
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:50,560
or so, it's likely that they'll tenure you almost for anything

331
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:52,440
from what I've seen.

332
00:13:52,440 --> 00:13:53,240
Just keep in mind.

333
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:54,280
Not economics, of course.

334
00:13:54,280 --> 00:13:55,240
We have high standards.

335
00:13:55,240 --> 00:13:57,920
But all these other departments, low, low standards.

336
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:00,760
And standard argument for not against the raise

337
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:02,840
of the standards is other universities at our level

338
00:14:02,840 --> 00:14:04,800
do the same thing.

339
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:07,120
And I made the reply, how will we rise above this level

340
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:08,760
if we don't raise standards?

341
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:10,320
That's not our concern.

342
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:11,720
Well, who's concerned about that?

343
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:13,560
It seems like we're the gatekeepers.

344
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:15,600
Now, no one listened.

345
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:19,240
Not on the committee.

346
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:22,040
Anyway, so yeah, if all employers think this way,

347
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:26,000
then they all raise wages above the market-bearing level,

348
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,680
which causes a permanent labor surplus.

349
00:14:30,680 --> 00:14:32,600
Now, some people have misinterpreted this

350
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:34,920
as some kind of confirmation of the Marxist concept

351
00:14:34,920 --> 00:14:37,960
of the reserve army of the unemployed.

352
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:39,400
Here's the thing, though.

353
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:41,600
The whole point of the reserve army of the unemployed

354
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:43,720
is supposed to be to prevent wages from ever rising.

355
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:45,960
The whole idea here is that they're already too high.

356
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:48,640
So essentially, the Marxist story

357
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:51,080
goes and combines two incompatible ideas.

358
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:52,760
One, that wages are permanently low.

359
00:14:52,760 --> 00:14:54,960
First of all, it's a model explaining why wages are

360
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:55,800
permanently low.

361
00:14:55,800 --> 00:14:57,500
And second of all, it's a model explaining

362
00:14:57,500 --> 00:14:59,120
why there's permanent unemployment.

363
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:01,540
But the only reason why there'd be permanent unemployment

364
00:15:01,540 --> 00:15:03,080
is if wages are generally too high.

365
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:08,960
And now, so then why is it someone

366
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:12,280
doesn't go and take advantage of this big group

367
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:14,080
of perfectly good workers?

368
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:15,840
At least in a simple version of the model,

369
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:19,040
these surplus workers are just as good as the employed ones.

370
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:22,800
There's no difference other than luck.

371
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:26,080
And the reason why is that given this asymmetric information

372
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:29,000
problem, if you offer to hire these other workers for lower

373
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,560
wages, their productivity falls at a faster rate

374
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:33,680
than the wage cut.

375
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:37,360
So if you say, hey, I'm happy to hire you for 20% less

376
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:41,000
than the current wage, and people say, yes,

377
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:45,640
we'll have productivity 25% less than normal,

378
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:47,080
you say, fine, I'll cut it again.

379
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:48,600
Productivity falls even faster.

380
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:51,680
So it's just like the market for lemons, right?

381
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:53,800
Why can't I just go and cut the price

382
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:55,480
that I'm offering for the used car?

383
00:15:55,480 --> 00:15:56,940
Because then the cars that you get

384
00:15:56,940 --> 00:15:58,440
will be worth even worse than that.

385
00:15:58,440 --> 00:16:01,960
So that's not going to work.

386
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:02,960
All right.

387
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:05,160
Now, efficiency wage models often somehow

388
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:06,920
assume to be the same as this.

389
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:08,340
And they're totally not the same.

390
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:10,160
They have nothing to do with each other.

391
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:14,040
You can combine both models, but models exist totally separately.

392
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:16,240
And standard efficiency wage models

393
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:19,200
are totally real models that have nothing to do with inflation.

394
00:16:19,200 --> 00:16:21,000
So keep that in mind.

395
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:21,600
All right.

396
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:24,800
Now, what is the point of these efficiency wage models?

397
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:28,200
Well, if you go into the math, it's

398
00:16:28,200 --> 00:16:30,720
easy to show that at least sometimes it's

399
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:32,960
going to be an inefficient outcome.

400
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:35,160
So obviously, this is going to be counter-efficient,

401
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:39,540
because these people would be happy to work for less,

402
00:16:39,540 --> 00:16:44,720
as long as we knew how good they were.

403
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:47,360
So as long as we actually knew what's going on,

404
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:48,160
they would be happy.

405
00:16:48,160 --> 00:16:50,240
So basically, they would be willing to go and put

406
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:55,800
in a full day's work in exchange for a below-market wage.

407
00:16:55,800 --> 00:16:58,000
However, since there's no way to actually enforce

408
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,480
that contract without having a higher wage,

409
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:03,080
then they don't do it.

410
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:05,680
So definitely counter-efficient, but even

411
00:17:05,680 --> 00:17:07,760
in some second-best sense, you can

412
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:09,640
show conditions under which you're

413
00:17:09,640 --> 00:17:12,960
getting an inefficient result.

414
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:14,240
So then what's the point?

415
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:17,160
Well, efficiency wages have been used as a rationale

416
00:17:17,160 --> 00:17:21,920
for industrial policy, usually for things

417
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:25,760
like subsidizing Rust Belt, subsidizing manufacturing,

418
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:29,720
on the theory that these are industries where the efficiency

419
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:32,480
wages are especially big.

420
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:35,920
So the efficiency wage issue is particularly severe.

421
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:37,800
Now, again, if you think about it more, it's a little bit

422
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:38,300
confusing.

423
00:17:38,300 --> 00:17:41,200
It's like, well, wait, why is it so hard to monitor productivity

424
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:43,880
on an auto assembly line, but it's

425
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:49,120
easy to monitor efficiency on a potato assembly line?

426
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:51,760
There's a famous model that people talk about it

427
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:54,480
a ton more, rather, on their motto,

428
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:56,520
on their slogan that in the 80s, when

429
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:58,880
some of the Reagan administration says,

430
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:01,200
it doesn't really matter whether we make potato ships

431
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:05,000
or silicon ships, all the same thing.

432
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,440
And so then they're widely criticized.

433
00:18:08,440 --> 00:18:09,840
But again, in terms of this model,

434
00:18:09,840 --> 00:18:13,240
you'd have to say that there's somehow worse information

435
00:18:13,240 --> 00:18:18,280
about the productivity of a potato chip maker

436
00:18:18,280 --> 00:18:20,800
than about the silicon chip maker.

437
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:24,200
It's kind of hard to understand by that point.

438
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:28,200
But anyway, if you were in a very similar model where

439
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:30,560
it was just like, you really could just be one industry.

440
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:33,160
But anyway, the idea is that you could do something

441
00:18:33,160 --> 00:18:37,640
like do a lump sum tax on everyone who has a job

442
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:41,200
and then use it to create a wage subsidy.

443
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:44,520
So that, potentially, could go and reduce the efficiency

444
00:18:44,520 --> 00:18:45,360
of that.

445
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,320
Although, once you're in that mode,

446
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:52,240
then you realize, wow, there's a lot of things

447
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:54,400
we could actually do to reduce this unemployment.

448
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:56,640
In fact, anything that makes unemployment less pleasant

449
00:18:56,640 --> 00:18:59,680
reduces unemployment in this model.

450
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:01,640
So remember, the reason why unemployment exists

451
00:19:01,640 --> 00:19:04,960
is it needs to be bad enough to scare people.

452
00:19:04,960 --> 00:19:07,040
So one way you can make unemployment

453
00:19:07,040 --> 00:19:11,080
bad enough to scare people is to get the unemployment rate at 20%.

454
00:19:11,080 --> 00:19:16,240
Another way, though, would be to make unemployment terrible.

455
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:20,440
And what are some simple ways to make unemployment terrible?

456
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:21,760
Cut unemployment insurance.

457
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:23,880
Yeah, so cut unemployment insurance.

458
00:19:23,880 --> 00:19:26,320
Or we could increase the stigma against people

459
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:28,200
that don't have a job.

460
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:30,660
You could have there be public service announcements saying,

461
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:32,400
are you dating an unemployed person?

462
00:19:32,400 --> 00:19:32,900
Don't.

463
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:34,840
Stay away from him.

464
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:38,320
He's a dirty man.

465
00:19:38,320 --> 00:19:41,120
Or there could be campaigns to educate parents.

466
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:43,640
If your kid loses a job, kick him out.

467
00:19:43,640 --> 00:19:45,360
Get rid of him.

468
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:49,440
A little time on the street will show him what's what.

469
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:52,160
Or, of course, you could even go full Soviet

470
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:55,240
and have a law against bankruptcy.

471
00:19:55,240 --> 00:19:58,440
So the standard thing is so a union, you've got to have a job.

472
00:19:58,440 --> 00:20:01,800
So if you don't have a job, then you can get a job.


1
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,420
a rest in remake industry, and then you go to a slave labor

2
00:20:02,420 --> 00:20:02,920
camp.

3
00:20:02,920 --> 00:20:03,840
So that's really bad.

4
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:06,000
None of this has got to be key to the legal system.

5
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,640
Just to point out, an interesting wage model

6
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:10,600
is usually seen as a left-wing model,

7
00:20:10,600 --> 00:20:13,760
but it immediately gives you an argument

8
00:20:13,760 --> 00:20:15,960
against unemployment insurance that

9
00:20:15,960 --> 00:20:18,880
exists on top of all the other ones about disincentives.

10
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:22,040
So of course, the standard one, that unemployment insurance

11
00:20:22,040 --> 00:20:23,880
reduces the incentive to get a job.

12
00:20:23,880 --> 00:20:26,200
But then there's this one saying it also just increases

13
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:30,920
the unemployment rate by making unemployment so pleasant

14
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:34,160
that you have to get more unemployment to make it scary

15
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:35,280
again.

16
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:37,520
And again, I'll suggest there's this kind of futility

17
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:41,760
in unemployment insurance because you go and give it,

18
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:44,160
and then that just causes the unemployment rate

19
00:20:44,160 --> 00:20:46,360
to go up in order to compensate to keep

20
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:47,720
the level of fear the same.

21
00:20:51,280 --> 00:20:53,760
So there we go.

22
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:56,840
So questions about efficiency wages?

23
00:20:56,840 --> 00:20:58,320
Copy efficiency wages?

24
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:03,780
Oh, yeah.

25
00:21:03,780 --> 00:21:07,200
In general, if you go and read textbook accounts

26
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:09,600
of efficiency wages, at least half of them

27
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:13,280
will just mess up and describe compensating differentials.

28
00:21:13,280 --> 00:21:15,240
So for example, not that you're likely to,

29
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:17,120
but if you go and get Dornbush and Fischer's

30
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:20,640
Intermediate Macroeconomics textbook from the early 90s,

31
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:22,400
I've got it on my shelf, they have

32
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:26,120
a discussion of efficiency wages that exactly describes

33
00:21:26,120 --> 00:21:27,800
compensating differentials.

34
00:21:27,800 --> 00:21:31,800
And when I went to Bill Dickens and said,

35
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:33,280
I said, look, there's no difference.

