00:21:33,280 --> 00:21:34,640
He said, you don't know what you're talking about, right?

37
00:21:34,640 --> 00:21:35,440
Of course there is.

38
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:36,720
And I showed him the textbook.

39
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:39,360
Look, these two fancy and famous guys from MIT,

40
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:41,000
they're saying exactly what I'm saying.

41
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,680
And Bill said, well, they're just wrong.

42
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:45,920
Oh, and then he went and showed me the math.

43
00:21:45,920 --> 00:21:47,320
I said, see, there is a difference.

44
00:21:47,320 --> 00:21:50,480
All right, well, why didn't they say that?

45
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:53,680
Great question, so possibly a case

46
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:56,840
where people were very, you know,

47
00:21:56,840 --> 00:21:59,920
really like a standard argument for using math in economics

48
00:21:59,920 --> 00:22:01,880
is you can express yourself clearly.

49
00:22:01,880 --> 00:22:04,320
But there's the problem, like all the back translation

50
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:05,760
problem of when you do all the math

51
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:08,120
and then try to explain the result in English again,

