00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:19,200
efficiency wages.

57
00:22:19,200 --> 00:22:22,000
So I say my version better than their version without the math

58
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:25,320
because what I said at least makes sense

59
00:22:25,320 --> 00:22:27,520
and was different from composition differentials.

60
00:22:27,520 --> 00:22:28,640
So which is the whole point of the model

61
00:22:28,640 --> 00:22:31,920
is to say something different from the really easy model

62
00:22:31,920 --> 00:22:36,040
that everyone should know from HRE.

63
00:22:36,040 --> 00:22:38,320
All right, we're now ready to move on

64
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:41,160
to finance and portfolio theory.

65
00:22:41,160 --> 00:22:44,360
All right, so let's start with permanent income anomalies.

66
00:22:44,360 --> 00:22:45,800
First of all, who here is diminishing

67
00:22:45,800 --> 00:22:48,280
marginal utility consumption?

68
00:22:48,280 --> 00:22:50,560
All right, most people have diminishing marginal utility

69
00:22:50,560 --> 00:22:51,760
consumption.

70
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:54,400
Who here has access to intertemporal markets,

71
00:22:54,400 --> 00:22:55,920
such as a credit card?

72
00:22:55,920 --> 00:22:58,920
All right, or could get a credit card if you wanted one.

73
00:22:58,920 --> 00:23:00,720
If these two things are true, then it

74
00:23:00,720 --> 00:23:03,200
makes very little sense to tailor your current consumption

75
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:05,880
to your current income.

76
00:23:05,880 --> 00:23:10,400
Why should you base what you are consuming now

77
00:23:10,400 --> 00:23:12,760
on how much money you are taking in right now when

78
00:23:12,760 --> 00:23:14,840
you could just go and borrow?

79
00:23:14,840 --> 00:23:16,400
So assume in grad school your income

80
00:23:16,400 --> 00:23:18,680
is low compared to what you expect for the rest of your

81
00:23:18,680 --> 00:23:20,960
life, so why not just go into debt,

82
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:24,160
and then you can live nicely right now,

83
00:23:24,160 --> 00:23:25,660
and you'll have to pay it back later.

84
00:23:25,660 --> 00:23:28,160
But if you've got diminishing marginal utility consumption,

85
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:30,960
isn't it better to smooth it out so that you

86
00:23:30,960 --> 00:23:33,360
have an intermediate lifestyle for your whole career

87
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:35,880
rather than living in poverty now

88
00:23:35,880 --> 00:23:38,040
and then living in luxury later?

89
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:43,080
So the smart thing to do is to base your consumption

90
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:47,720
on your permanent or expected long-run income.

91
00:23:47,720 --> 00:23:52,240
So your permanent income, expected long-run income.

92
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:54,740
And actually, this is the main thing that won Milton Friedman

93
00:23:54,740 --> 00:23:55,400
his Nobel Prize.

94
00:23:55,400 --> 00:23:58,240
Not monetarism, not creaches, not that stuff,

95
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:01,080
but his work in the 50s on the permanent income hypothesis.

96
00:24:01,080 --> 00:24:03,120
That was what really won him the Nobel Prize.

97
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:05,920
It's the main thing that got cited.

98
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:08,440
And obviously, there's a lot of truth

99
00:24:08,440 --> 00:24:10,920
in this permanent income hypothesis.

100
00:24:10,920 --> 00:24:13,680
We don't know if this is that people base their consumption

101
00:24:13,680 --> 00:24:15,440
on their expected long-run income

102
00:24:15,440 --> 00:24:20,360
rather than what they happen to have earned this week.

103
00:24:20,360 --> 00:24:23,040
So for example, young people tend to build up a lot of debt,

104
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:25,560
and they also tend to pay it off as they age.

105
00:24:25,560 --> 00:24:27,280
Well, none of that.

106
00:24:27,280 --> 00:24:29,120
And then when people retire, they generally

107
00:24:29,120 --> 00:24:33,960
consume out of assets they built up when they were working.

108
00:24:33,960 --> 00:24:36,120
All pretty well in fact.

109
00:24:36,120 --> 00:24:39,320
Experienced this before.

110
00:24:39,320 --> 00:24:41,720
But when behavioral economists have

111
00:24:41,720 --> 00:24:45,920
taken a closer look at the world of consumption and savings,

112
00:24:45,920 --> 00:24:47,600
they come up with a bunch of violations

113
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:49,440
of the permanent income hypothesis.

114
00:24:49,440 --> 00:24:51,640
So at least it doesn't seem to work perfectly.

115
00:24:51,640 --> 00:24:54,520
In fact, fairly far from perfectly.

116
00:24:54,520 --> 00:24:56,880
So for one thing, consumption does actually

117
00:24:56,880 --> 00:25:00,160
seem moderately sensitive to current income.

118
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:01,960
So we look at medical students.

119
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:04,200
Medical students have an enormously high graduation

120
00:25:04,200 --> 00:25:04,920
rate.

121
00:25:04,920 --> 00:25:07,920
It's like 95% of people that start medical school

122
00:25:07,920 --> 00:25:10,480
finish medical school.

123
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:12,320
Really high.

124
00:25:12,320 --> 00:25:13,680
And doctors, you may have heard.

125
00:25:13,680 --> 00:25:15,760
And of course, if you would finish medical school,

126
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:17,560
then you're very likely to become a doctor.

127
00:25:17,560 --> 00:25:21,400
And doctors are very likely to have very high income.

128
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:23,480
So given that there's not much uncertainty that

129
00:25:23,480 --> 00:25:26,560
gets resolved during this process,

130
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:28,680
you would think that medical students would just

131
00:25:28,680 --> 00:25:31,120
rack up piles of debt.

132
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:32,600
And not just for medicals.

133
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:35,960
Not the obvious one, where I can't get for medical school.

134
00:25:35,960 --> 00:25:37,560
That's a given.

135
00:25:37,560 --> 00:25:39,560
So why don't you go and rack up lots of debt

136
00:25:39,560 --> 00:25:42,040
to eat at nice restaurants when you're in medical school?

137
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:44,560
And have a nice car and live in a nice apartment.

138
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:47,640
Why not go and rack up debt for all of these things,

139
00:25:47,640 --> 00:25:50,680
and then pay it off out of your $300,000 or $400,000 a year

140
00:25:50,680 --> 00:25:53,160
salary once you're done?

141
00:25:53,160 --> 00:25:54,360
Why not?

142
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:57,080
Why not?

143
00:25:57,080 --> 00:25:59,520
But when people actually look at what medical students do,

144
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:01,720
they see that there is a very predictable rise

145
00:26:01,720 --> 00:26:05,480
in consumption right when they start working.

146
00:26:05,480 --> 00:26:06,960
So you start working, and then it's

147
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:11,280
like, oh, fine, time to go and buy a nice car, buy nice stuff,

148
00:26:11,280 --> 00:26:14,440
get a nice apartment, now that I finished medical school.

149
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:16,240
In fact, this is such a standard reaction

150
00:26:16,240 --> 00:26:19,200
that we don't really think about it as weird.

151
00:26:19,200 --> 00:26:20,880
But it is weird.

152
00:26:20,880 --> 00:26:22,440
One thing, if finishing medical school

153
00:26:22,440 --> 00:26:24,640
was like a one in 100 event, then

154
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:26,840
finishing medical school is like winning the lottery.

155
00:26:26,840 --> 00:26:29,960
Then it does make sense to go and ramp up your lifestyle

156
00:26:29,960 --> 00:26:32,320
a lot if you happen to finish.

157
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:35,440
But if you're almost certain to finish, then it's weird.

158
00:26:35,440 --> 00:26:38,800
So it was almost certain that it was going to happen.

159
00:26:38,800 --> 00:26:42,400
So why weren't you already living like a doctor?

160
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:44,000
Why not live like a doctor early?

161
00:26:47,560 --> 00:26:50,480
And so I'm as guilty of this as anyone.

162
00:26:50,480 --> 00:26:54,560
So when I was in grad school, I lived so poorly

163
00:26:54,560 --> 00:26:56,000
in terms of materially.

164
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,720
So there was one where actually it was so dumb,

165
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:02,040
you can't even explain it by current income, by deviation.

166
00:27:02,040 --> 00:27:05,480
So the day after I got married, I changed my own oil on a day

167
00:27:05,480 --> 00:27:07,520
we were supposed to be packing for the honeymoon.

168
00:27:07,520 --> 00:27:09,480
And so we didn't wind up leaving until midnight

169
00:27:09,480 --> 00:27:12,720
because I am not actually good at changing oil.

170
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:14,440
And it was a really hard day.

171
00:27:14,440 --> 00:27:18,320
And this is one where we had a giant bag of cash gifts.

172
00:27:18,320 --> 00:27:20,440
And I could have simply gone in there

173
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:24,680
and taken 1% of that money out and taken it to Jiffy Lube

174
00:27:24,680 --> 00:27:26,200
and had it done.

175
00:27:26,200 --> 00:27:28,800
The only excuse I can plead is that I

176
00:27:28,800 --> 00:27:31,520
was raised in a family where no one ever changed.

177
00:27:31,520 --> 00:27:33,280
My dad always changed our oil.

178
00:27:33,280 --> 00:27:35,440
And so I was just not really familiar with the idea

179
00:27:35,440 --> 00:27:37,000
of paying people for this stuff.

180
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:38,240
Oh, you didn't.

181
00:27:38,240 --> 00:27:40,120
If Sarbhati showed up and I said,

182
00:27:40,120 --> 00:27:43,920
you are aware there are places that do this, right?

183
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:46,200
Then I wouldn't have said no.

184
00:27:46,200 --> 00:27:47,000
I didn't know that.

185
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:48,000
I would have said yes.

186
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,280
And you realize that you are miserable now.

187
00:27:50,280 --> 00:27:52,120
Yes, I know.

188
00:27:52,120 --> 00:27:55,600
You realize that there is a big basket of money in there.

189
00:27:55,600 --> 00:27:56,560
Yes.

190
00:27:56,560 --> 00:27:58,840
So do you see where I'm going with this?

191
00:27:58,840 --> 00:28:01,000
And I think I would have done it if someone had just

192
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:02,760
come and helped me in this way.

193
00:28:02,760 --> 00:28:04,840
And I kept changing my own oil in grad school

194
00:28:04,840 --> 00:28:06,280
for a while, actually.

195
00:28:06,280 --> 00:28:08,200
I blame my friends for not making fun of me

196
00:28:08,200 --> 00:28:10,160
for my economic illiteracy.

197
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:12,040
If one of them had walked by and just said,

198
00:28:12,040 --> 00:28:17,320
ha ha, you're working for less than minimum wage,

199
00:28:17,320 --> 00:28:18,960
that would have been a big help to me.

200
00:28:18,960 --> 00:28:21,120
But no one did it.

201
00:28:21,120 --> 00:28:24,520
And so I worked for less than minimum wage,

202
00:28:24,520 --> 00:28:29,720
changing oil, which is almost the worst job I've ever had.

203
00:28:29,720 --> 00:28:34,920
Anyway, so this is something where I am a sinner right now.

204
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:36,800
Repented, but.

205
00:28:36,800 --> 00:28:39,160
So I'm helping you now.

206
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:40,760
So just imagine me laughing at you

207
00:28:40,760 --> 00:28:42,960
if you're working for less than minimum wage,

208
00:28:42,960 --> 00:28:44,600
doing something that you don't enjoy.

209
00:28:47,880 --> 00:28:49,480
Anyway, consumption does seem moderately

210
00:28:49,480 --> 00:28:50,960
sensitive to current income.

211
00:28:50,960 --> 00:28:52,560
There's an old paper by Greg Mankiw

212
00:28:52,560 --> 00:28:56,160
where he estimates a model where half the people just

213
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:58,920
consume whatever they consume out of current income

214
00:28:58,920 --> 00:29:00,560
and half do permanent income.

215
00:29:00,560 --> 00:29:01,520
So essentially, it's one where there's

216
00:29:01,520 --> 00:29:03,520
just two types of consumers, in other words.

217
00:29:03,520 --> 00:29:05,600
Realistically, there'd be a continuum of people.

218
00:29:05,600 --> 00:29:07,560
But anyway, in a model where you assume

219
00:29:07,560 --> 00:29:10,760
that either you are a dummy who just mechanically consumes

220
00:29:10,760 --> 00:29:13,400
out of current income or you're Milton Friedman,

221
00:29:13,400 --> 00:29:15,960
then his estimate is half the population is Milton Friedman

222
00:29:15,960 --> 00:29:17,800
and half is dumb.

223
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:22,000
So it gives you some idea.

224
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,280
Further result is that consumption responses seem

225
00:29:24,280 --> 00:29:27,040
to vary with the nature of the income.

226
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:29,680
Consumption responses vary the nature of the income.

227
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:32,920
So when people get a one-time cash bonus,

228
00:29:32,920 --> 00:29:37,320
then they are likely to use it to buy a one-time big cash

229
00:29:37,320 --> 00:29:39,200
thing.

230
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:40,360
So you get a bonus.

231
00:29:40,360 --> 00:29:42,080
A lot of people just take the whole bonus

232
00:29:42,080 --> 00:29:45,440
and buy something with it, which again, is not

233
00:29:45,440 --> 00:29:48,440
what the permanent income hypothesis would predict.

234
00:29:48,440 --> 00:29:50,720
Normal prediction would be that if you get a cash

235
00:29:50,720 --> 00:29:53,560
bonus of $1,000 you didn't expect,

236
00:29:53,560 --> 00:29:56,440
this will have the same effect as if your expected lifetime

237
00:29:56,440 --> 00:30:00,720
income goes up by that amount of present value.

238
00:30:00,720 --> 00:30:02,080
Yes?

239
00:30:02,080 --> 00:30:05,040
Why can't you explain something like the medical school

240
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:08,240
thing with sort of like an expected value kind of thing?

241
00:30:08,240 --> 00:30:11,600
Like the probability that you don't finish medical school

242
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:13,080
might be really small.

243
00:30:13,080 --> 00:30:16,160
But if it happens and you spent, you took out a whole bunch

244
00:30:16,160 --> 00:30:19,000
of loans to live lavishly while you're in,

245
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,280
like you're really screwed and your life's going to be

246
00:30:21,280 --> 00:30:26,040
terrible, but how bad it is to just sort of not

247
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:27,740
eat at a nice restaurant for a few years

248
00:30:27,740 --> 00:30:31,160
while you're in medical school isn't nearly probably as bad

249
00:30:31,160 --> 00:30:34,280
as the rest of your life would be if you have all that debt

250
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:37,360
and for whatever reason you're in the 5% that doesn't finish.

251
00:30:37,360 --> 00:30:37,760
Yeah.

252
00:30:37,760 --> 00:30:40,160
So the answer there is just like with the expected utility

253
00:30:40,160 --> 00:30:41,880
theory in general, the problem is

254
00:30:41,880 --> 00:30:45,040
there seems to be this excess sensitivity to a few percent

255
00:30:45,040 --> 00:30:46,280
risk.

256
00:30:46,280 --> 00:30:48,560
So it seems like, again, not much difference

257
00:30:48,560 --> 00:30:50,960
in the response between someone who has a 1% chance of not

258
00:30:50,960 --> 00:30:53,160
finishing and a 5% or a 10% or a 20%.

259
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:56,040
In both cases, you finish and then cha-ching,

260
00:30:56,040 --> 00:30:59,640
whereas if it were just a simple expected utility model,

261
00:30:59,640 --> 00:31:00,960
then you're right.

262
00:31:00,960 --> 00:31:03,320
There would be some response, but it

263
00:31:03,320 --> 00:31:05,760
should depend upon how much uncertainty is being resolved.

264
00:31:05,760 --> 00:31:08,720
And that seems like there's a big range where

265
00:31:08,720 --> 00:31:09,760
there's any difference.

266
00:31:09,760 --> 00:31:11,560
And again, remember that just the fact

267
00:31:11,560 --> 00:31:15,680
that you're a doctor does not actually guarantee you anything.

268
00:31:15,680 --> 00:31:18,120
And yet people sort of act like, well, now I'm a doctor.

269
00:31:18,120 --> 00:31:19,200
Now everything is sure.

270
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:20,880
It's like, well, what if you lose your license

271
00:31:20,880 --> 00:31:22,160
because you made a mistake?

272
00:31:22,160 --> 00:31:23,660
And then you build up a lot of debt,

273
00:31:23,660 --> 00:31:25,400
and then you're in big trouble.

274
00:31:25,400 --> 00:31:28,200
But that one doesn't seem to bother people the same way.

275
00:31:28,200 --> 00:31:28,840
Yes.

276
00:31:28,840 --> 00:31:30,800
So again, there's these pseudo-certainty effects

277
00:31:30,800 --> 00:31:33,960
where you reach a social milestone of, I finished.

278
00:31:33,960 --> 00:31:35,080
Now I am a doctor.

279
00:31:35,080 --> 00:31:37,000
Now I'm entitled to spend.

280
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:39,720
It's like, well, yeah, but so many things could go wrong.

281
00:31:39,720 --> 00:31:42,560
You could get in a terrible accident.

282
00:31:42,560 --> 00:31:45,480
Like if you're a surgeon, your hands could get paralyzed

283
00:31:45,480 --> 00:31:46,960
and just list off all the things.

284
00:31:46,960 --> 00:31:52,120
So even then, there'd still be reasons for you to go and take

285
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:53,160
extra precautions.

286
00:31:53,160 --> 00:31:54,920
And yet there's a lot of these where

287
00:31:54,920 --> 00:32:00,080
people seem to not be planning much at all, if at all.

288
00:32:00,080 --> 00:32:01,240
All right.

289
00:32:01,240 --> 00:32:02,080
Now, yes.

290
00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:04,560
You used to say that people are loss averse.

291
00:32:04,560 --> 00:32:07,880
So in the back, they usually take a larger risk

292
00:32:07,880 --> 00:32:11,040
than they exactly have.

293
00:32:11,040 --> 00:32:13,720
So actually, what I said is that many behavioral economists

294
00:32:13,720 --> 00:32:15,720
think that people are loss averse.

295
00:32:15,720 --> 00:32:18,960
Yeah, so again, that would mean that, yes.

296
00:32:18,960 --> 00:32:21,080
So actually, that seems strange.

297
00:32:21,080 --> 00:32:22,960
Because remember, what loss aversion

298
00:32:22,960 --> 00:32:25,960
says is that you'll be risk preferring for losses.

299
00:32:25,960 --> 00:32:27,560
So actually, this should mean that you

300
00:32:27,560 --> 00:32:30,840
shouldn't be worrying about a small chance

301
00:32:30,840 --> 00:32:33,240
that you don't finish medical school.

302
00:32:33,240 --> 00:32:34,720
At least that's the simple version.

303
00:32:34,720 --> 00:32:36,920
And remember, there's the fourfold version,

304
00:32:36,920 --> 00:32:40,160
which makes it more complicated.

305
00:32:40,160 --> 00:32:41,400
All right.

306
00:32:41,400 --> 00:32:42,560
Yes.

307
00:32:42,560 --> 00:32:45,760
So it is possible that a better life

308
00:32:45,760 --> 00:32:49,320
is that kind of life purpose.

309
00:32:49,320 --> 00:32:54,840
If I already live very well now, I lose my purpose of living.

310
00:32:54,840 --> 00:32:56,760
So I would like to leave some space

311
00:32:56,760 --> 00:33:00,360
that will work harder to reach a better life in the future.

312
00:33:00,360 --> 00:33:00,920
Right, right.

313
00:33:00,920 --> 00:33:03,400
So that means that you're sort of getting,

314
00:33:03,400 --> 00:33:05,360
at least in a general sense, you're

315
00:33:05,360 --> 00:33:06,880
getting rid of a simple diminishing

316
00:33:06,880 --> 00:33:08,880
marginal utility consumption model.

317
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:11,280
And instead, it's one where at any point in time

318
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:12,400
it's diminishing.

319
00:33:12,400 --> 00:33:14,360
But over my life, I want it to be rising.

320
00:33:14,360 --> 00:33:16,920
So I feel like my life is improving.

321
00:33:16,920 --> 00:33:20,080
So that again would give you a very different model

322
00:33:20,080 --> 00:33:20,880
than the usual one.

323
00:33:20,880 --> 00:33:24,440
But that makes quite a bit of sense

324
00:33:24,440 --> 00:33:27,840
as a description of people's behavior.

325
00:33:27,840 --> 00:33:30,080
All right, now if you have durable goods,

326
00:33:30,080 --> 00:33:32,720
this gives you a little bit of extra room.

327
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:34,400
But even there, not too much.

328
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:35,860
Again, so when you add durable goods

329
00:33:35,860 --> 00:33:37,760
into the permanent income hypothesis,

330
00:33:37,760 --> 00:33:41,800
this says that it's basically an optimal stock of durable goods

331
00:33:41,800 --> 00:33:45,000
that you want to have given whatever your expected lifetime

332
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:46,320
income is.

333
00:33:46,320 --> 00:33:49,160
And this means that if suddenly there's some new news,

334
00:33:49,160 --> 00:33:51,680
you may want to go and buy nicer durable goods.

335
00:33:51,680 --> 00:33:53,140
But again, the problem is it seems

336
00:33:53,140 --> 00:33:56,000
like people also have this excess sensitivity

337
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:57,400
for current consumption.

338
00:33:57,400 --> 00:34:00,320
It's like going to restaurants and things like that.

339
00:34:00,320 --> 00:34:02,200
So it is true that if you find out

340
00:34:02,200 --> 00:34:04,560
that you're going to inherit some money, that is a reason

341
00:34:04,560 --> 00:34:06,840
to go and buy a car.

342
00:34:06,840 --> 00:34:08,840
Or even if you find out that you're

343
00:34:08,840 --> 00:34:11,040
going to get a 10% raise, that is a reason

344
00:34:11,040 --> 00:34:13,120
to go out and buy a new car today because you see,

345
00:34:13,120 --> 00:34:16,120
well, now it makes sense for my stock of durable goods

346
00:34:16,120 --> 00:34:17,920
to go up.

347
00:34:17,920 --> 00:34:20,120
But anyway, so durable goods do make some difference.

348
00:34:20,120 --> 00:34:22,080
And with durable goods, this is the main way

349
00:34:22,080 --> 00:34:23,680
where in a simple model it can still

350
00:34:23,680 --> 00:34:26,600
make sense for you to run out and buy a really nice thing

351
00:34:26,600 --> 00:34:27,820
when you get a raise.

352
00:34:27,820 --> 00:34:29,280
But it's got to be a durable thing.

353
00:34:29,280 --> 00:34:30,600
So in the model, it doesn't make sense

354
00:34:30,600 --> 00:34:32,520
to go to a nice restaurant when you get a raise.

355
00:34:32,520 --> 00:34:35,240
It doesn't make sense to get a car or get a better car

356
00:34:35,240 --> 00:34:38,360
because the restaurant is the current consumption,

357
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:42,320
whereas the car is a durable good.

358
00:34:42,320 --> 00:34:44,720
And furthermore, also empirical evidence

359
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:48,120
that people are reluctant to borrow against or tap

360
00:34:48,120 --> 00:34:52,840
into pensions and home equity to smooth their current income.

361
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:55,360
So if you have home equity, if you have any home equity

362
00:34:55,360 --> 00:35:02,680
built up, you actually have a fantastic source of credit,

363
00:35:02,680 --> 00:35:04,960
which is you just borrow against your home equity.

364
00:35:04,960 --> 00:35:07,120
And the rate is ridiculously low,

365
00:35:07,120 --> 00:35:09,480
especially adjusting for taxes.

366
00:35:09,480 --> 00:35:13,720
So very common rate if you have a decent amount of home equity

367
00:35:13,720 --> 00:35:16,080
would be like prime rate minus a half.

368
00:35:16,080 --> 00:35:19,880
And then all that interest is deductible on your taxes.

369
00:35:19,880 --> 00:35:22,800
The Trump tax reform changed a little bit, but not that much.

370
00:35:22,800 --> 00:35:25,880
And so actually, there's a lot of people

371
00:35:25,880 --> 00:35:27,560
who effectively have a negative real rate

372
00:35:27,560 --> 00:35:30,320
they can borrow against up to an enormous amount of money,

373
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:31,480
especially if they've been older and they've

374
00:35:31,480 --> 00:35:32,920
been building up equity.

375
00:35:32,920 --> 00:35:34,840
Now, there was a question back there.

376
00:35:34,840 --> 00:35:38,280
Yeah, is that historically true?

377
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:41,160
Like pre-2007, say?

378
00:35:41,160 --> 00:35:45,080
That you could do this, that it was possible to go and borrow

379
00:35:45,080 --> 00:35:46,640
against home equity?

380
00:35:46,640 --> 00:35:48,120
No, no, no.

381
00:35:48,120 --> 00:35:51,000
Oh, that people are reluctant to do it?

382
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:51,520
Yeah.

383
00:35:51,520 --> 00:35:52,020
Yes.

384
00:35:52,020 --> 00:35:54,160
So historically, people were definitely reluctant.

385
00:35:54,160 --> 00:35:59,000
During the years up to the Great Recession,

386
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:01,320
it seemed like some of the psychological barriers

387
00:36:01,320 --> 00:36:03,040
were starting to fall a bit.

388
00:36:03,040 --> 00:36:04,960
And then as to how much they've been restored,

389
00:36:04,960 --> 00:36:07,640
I'm not clear on this.

390
00:36:07,640 --> 00:36:09,720
So anyway, there is this reluctance

391
00:36:09,720 --> 00:36:13,160
to tap into things that are called savings,

392
00:36:13,160 --> 00:36:17,480
even when it's a really low interest rate.

393
00:36:17,480 --> 00:36:19,800
And you could say, well, yeah, it's called savings.

394
00:36:19,800 --> 00:36:22,760
But what's the difference between something

395
00:36:22,760 --> 00:36:25,280
called savings and something not called savings if they're

396
00:36:25,280 --> 00:36:26,360
both the same?

397
00:36:26,360 --> 00:36:30,600
And again, there are people who are so silly as to have,

398
00:36:30,600 --> 00:36:35,280
say, $30,000 in credit card debt and $100,000 in home equity.

399
00:36:35,280 --> 00:36:37,560
And there's a very simple financial transaction

400
00:36:37,560 --> 00:36:40,760
you can make to greatly reduce the amount of interest

401
00:36:40,760 --> 00:36:43,120
you have to pay while maintaining everything else.

402
00:36:43,120 --> 00:36:45,240
So if you've got $100,000 in home equity, $30,000

403
00:36:45,240 --> 00:36:47,960
in credit card debt, what is the simple operation

404
00:36:47,960 --> 00:36:51,360
that you would be well advised to try?

405
00:36:51,360 --> 00:36:53,360
Use the home equity to pay off your credit card.

406
00:36:53,360 --> 00:36:53,360
Yes.

407
00:36:53,360 --> 00:36:55,060
Borrow $30,000 to get your home equity,

408
00:36:55,060 --> 00:36:57,880
pay off your credit card.

409
00:36:57,880 --> 00:37:00,840
Because the credit card debt interest rate is,

410
00:37:00,840 --> 00:37:07,520
in real terms, actually, maybe 1,000 times the rates.

411
00:37:07,520 --> 00:37:09,080
That you are paying on home equity.

412
00:37:09,080 --> 00:37:10,800
Home equity really could be a negative.

413
00:37:10,800 --> 00:37:12,960
Actually, it could easily be a negative rate.

414
00:37:12,960 --> 00:37:16,240
And the credit card, probably a 15% rate.

415
00:37:16,240 --> 00:37:18,120
So and yet, well, why not?

416
00:37:18,120 --> 00:37:20,440
Well, but that's my home equity.

417
00:37:20,440 --> 00:37:23,560
That's like my special status of protection.

418
00:37:23,560 --> 00:37:25,120
And this credit card, well, that's

419
00:37:25,120 --> 00:37:28,160
the monument to my profligacy.

420
00:37:28,160 --> 00:37:33,440
But let's keep it there to remind me of my sin.

421
00:37:33,440 --> 00:37:35,280
And again, so you guys are familiar with what

422
00:37:35,280 --> 00:37:37,240
a reverse mortgage is?

423
00:37:37,240 --> 00:37:38,800
Yeah, so there's some comedians who

424
00:37:38,800 --> 00:37:40,400
have talked about it in general.

425
00:37:40,400 --> 00:37:41,920
But they make it sound stupid.

426
00:37:41,920 --> 00:37:43,680
But it's actually a really good idea.

427
00:37:43,680 --> 00:37:45,600
And with a normal mortgage, there's

428
00:37:45,600 --> 00:37:50,120
a person who wants a house, but they don't have money.

429
00:37:50,120 --> 00:37:52,820
So they go and they say, look, if you give me a house,

430
00:37:52,820 --> 00:37:55,680
I will agree to go and pay the bank money every month

431
00:37:55,680 --> 00:37:58,000
for 20 or 30 years.

432
00:37:58,000 --> 00:38:01,600
And with a reverse mortgage, you start out owning a house.

433
00:38:01,600 --> 00:38:03,640
And you want the bank to give you money.

434
00:38:03,640 --> 00:38:05,200
So you say, tell you what, you give me

435
00:38:05,200 --> 00:38:06,360
money for 20 or 30 years.

436
00:38:06,360 --> 00:38:07,960
And then at the end, you get a house.

437
00:38:07,960 --> 00:38:09,540
Usually, they're structured so that you

438
00:38:09,540 --> 00:38:11,120
get money until you die.

439
00:38:11,120 --> 00:38:13,560
And then the bank gets the house.

440
00:38:13,560 --> 00:38:16,080
And then normally, of course, they have extra revisions.

441
00:38:16,080 --> 00:38:17,800
Like if you die in a month, then the bank

442
00:38:17,800 --> 00:38:19,000
doesn't get the entire house.

443
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:21,040
Although there's a good reason to actually sign

444
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:23,160
a contract saying the bank can get the entire house

445
00:38:23,160 --> 00:38:26,080
if you die next month, which is they'll give you more money.

446
00:38:26,080 --> 00:38:28,160
They'll give you more money if they get this more

447
00:38:28,160 --> 00:38:29,240
favorable contract.

448
00:38:29,240 --> 00:38:32,720
Anyway, so reverse mortgage has originally been very unpopular.

449
00:38:32,720 --> 00:38:35,800
And even for the people who make a lot of sense for them

450
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:38,840
to do those, to get them, it's like elderly people

451
00:38:38,840 --> 00:38:42,400
who are living very modestly, sometimes eating dog food,

452
00:38:42,400 --> 00:38:45,640
in homes of great value.

453
00:38:45,640 --> 00:38:47,640
So put that together, and the reverse mortgage

454
00:38:47,640 --> 00:38:49,720
makes a great deal of sense.

455
00:38:49,720 --> 00:38:51,100
This is another case, by the way,

456
00:38:51,100 --> 00:38:53,840
where the regulatory response to people underusing

457
00:38:53,840 --> 00:38:56,920
reverse mortgages has been to try to prevent

458
00:38:56,920 --> 00:38:59,240
more reverse mortgages.

459
00:38:59,240 --> 00:39:00,880
So government regulators generally

460
00:39:00,880 --> 00:39:02,600
have the view that only a dummy would ever

461
00:39:02,600 --> 00:39:05,160
want a reverse mortgage because you're losing a house.

462
00:39:05,160 --> 00:39:07,360
And so you're just taking advantage of the elderly.

463
00:39:07,360 --> 00:39:09,160
Rather, the more sensible view of it's just

464
00:39:09,160 --> 00:39:11,560
stubborn elderly people who don't realize reverse mortgage

465
00:39:11,560 --> 00:39:13,720
isn't right for them, which I would say

466
00:39:13,720 --> 00:39:16,000
would be a more reasonable thing would be for regulators

467
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:18,560
to be trying to ram the reverse mortgages down people's

468
00:39:18,560 --> 00:39:21,120
throats and sending them things saying,

469
00:39:21,120 --> 00:39:24,040
unless you fill out these, if you write an essay explaining

470
00:39:24,040 --> 00:39:26,920
why you don't want one, you get one.

471
00:39:26,920 --> 00:39:28,400
And then it gets graded by someone

472
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:29,600
on your financial literacy.

473
00:39:29,600 --> 00:39:31,520
So just make sure that you understand

474
00:39:31,520 --> 00:39:34,480
what a great opportunity you're missing out on.

475
00:39:34,480 --> 00:39:35,600
Probably some of it is people's kids

476
00:39:35,600 --> 00:39:37,480
are mad that their parents get to live nicely

477
00:39:37,480 --> 00:39:39,720
and they don't get to inherit a house.

478
00:39:39,720 --> 00:39:42,720
Those are called bad kids.

479
00:39:42,720 --> 00:39:44,920
Regretting this to your parents.

480
00:39:44,920 --> 00:39:51,880
So this does bring me to my very favorite conversation

481
00:39:51,880 --> 00:39:54,800
about my dad's economic illiteracy, reflective of it.

482
00:39:54,800 --> 00:39:59,600
So this is like around 2005, 2006.

483
00:39:59,600 --> 00:40:05,600
The homeless.


1
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:04,160
that my parents bought for $35,000 in 1972

2
00:40:04,160 --> 00:40:06,440
became worth over $1 million.

3
00:40:06,440 --> 00:40:07,280
This is all nominal.

4
00:40:07,280 --> 00:40:11,240
But yeah, so in Los Angeles, during the housing bubble,

5
00:40:11,240 --> 00:40:14,480
so the price of the house, it goes over $1 million.

6
00:40:14,480 --> 00:40:19,000
And my dad, as usual, is angry about it.

7
00:40:19,000 --> 00:40:21,480
So he's like, how many prankers have gone

8
00:40:21,480 --> 00:40:24,520
to places that have gone completely crazy, Brian?

9
00:40:24,520 --> 00:40:26,560
I'm like, well, why aren't you happy?

10
00:40:26,560 --> 00:40:31,120
You own one of the houses that has gone way up.

11
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:33,240
And then he goes, what good does it do me?

12
00:40:33,240 --> 00:40:34,680
Because I don't want to sell.

13
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:38,520
And I said, well, you could go and get a home equity

14
00:40:38,520 --> 00:40:41,840
loan against the extra value and spend it on anything you want.

15
00:40:41,840 --> 00:40:42,840
He goes, no, you can't.

16
00:40:42,840 --> 00:40:44,520
You can only use that row of improvement.

17
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:46,720
I said, well, that might have been true in the 1950s.

18
00:40:46,720 --> 00:40:48,680
But now you can spend it on anything you want.

19
00:40:48,680 --> 00:40:50,880
And then it only takes about five seconds.

20
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:52,380
He goes, what would I spend it on?

21
00:40:52,380 --> 00:40:57,320
I said, well, I don't know, you could give the money to me.

22
00:40:57,320 --> 00:40:58,280
I don't know.

23
00:40:58,280 --> 00:41:01,240
I don't know.

24
00:41:01,240 --> 00:41:04,080
So really, it started off with a complaint that made no sense

25
00:41:04,080 --> 00:41:07,520
and ended up with money does not help me anyway.

26
00:41:07,520 --> 00:41:09,880
So it's like, are you serious?

27
00:41:09,880 --> 00:41:14,080
There's really no winning in this conversation.

28
00:41:14,080 --> 00:41:17,600
Does it mean that actually your dad has perfectly rational

29
00:41:17,600 --> 00:41:19,120
purchasing preferences?

30
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:21,280
Because if he gets money, that means

31
00:41:21,280 --> 00:41:25,520
he has reached perfect marginal utility on everything.

32
00:41:25,520 --> 00:41:27,840
He has no idea what to do with the extra dollar.

33
00:41:27,840 --> 00:41:29,400
It's even worse.

34
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:31,840
The story's got zero marginal utility,

35
00:41:31,840 --> 00:41:35,280
marginal utility of additional consumption, which

36
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:36,640
almost describes him, actually.

37
00:41:36,640 --> 00:41:42,400
Yeah, I mean, now, my confession here

38
00:41:42,400 --> 00:41:44,640
is there is something very jerky about what I'm doing,

39
00:41:44,640 --> 00:41:47,400
which is he still works very hard earning money.

40
00:41:47,400 --> 00:41:49,920
And all of this, since he doesn't spend a dime on himself,

41
00:41:49,920 --> 00:41:52,120
will go to his descendants.

42
00:41:52,120 --> 00:41:55,880
But still, the story is just too good not to tell.

43
00:41:55,880 --> 00:41:58,680
Some people can be angry about any possible economic

44
00:41:58,680 --> 00:41:59,920
occurrence.

45
00:41:59,920 --> 00:42:02,840
And my dad, getting mad that his house

46
00:42:02,840 --> 00:42:05,520
was worth over a million dollars, was one of them.

47
00:42:08,760 --> 00:42:14,120
OK, so there is a standard neoclassical explanation

48
00:42:14,120 --> 00:42:16,920
of why the permanent income hypothesis fails.

49
00:42:16,920 --> 00:42:20,960
And this says that it is due to what are called liquidity

50
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:22,000
constraints.

51
00:42:25,680 --> 00:42:27,520
And there's totally something to this story.

52
00:42:27,520 --> 00:42:29,920
So this says, look, suppose you're in medical school,

53
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:32,360
and you go to a bank and say, I'm a medical student.

54
00:42:32,360 --> 00:42:33,920
I'm going to be a doctor pretty soon.

55
00:42:33,920 --> 00:42:38,960
So I'd like $300,000 cash now.

56
00:42:38,960 --> 00:42:42,480
How would most banks respond to this request?

57
00:42:42,480 --> 00:42:43,800
No.

58
00:42:43,800 --> 00:42:45,280
What collateral do you have?

59
00:42:45,280 --> 00:42:47,360
Look at it.

60
00:42:47,360 --> 00:42:48,400
Doctor material.

61
00:42:48,400 --> 00:42:49,440
Right here, baby.

62
00:42:49,440 --> 00:42:50,000
Right here.

63
00:42:50,000 --> 00:42:53,360
Like these hands, that's my collateral.

64
00:42:53,360 --> 00:42:57,040
So in general, banks would not be very interested in this.

65
00:42:57,040 --> 00:43:01,640
And the reason is that it seems that despite the fact

66
00:43:01,640 --> 00:43:03,360
you're lending to a doctor, still they

67
00:43:03,360 --> 00:43:06,960
are skeptical that they will get paid back in anything like full

68
00:43:06,960 --> 00:43:09,080
because it's an unsecured loan.

69
00:43:09,080 --> 00:43:14,000
They don't know there's no way they could garage your wages

70
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:14,960
or anything else.

71
00:43:14,960 --> 00:43:16,560
You could just declare bankruptcy.

72
00:43:16,560 --> 00:43:19,800
And so they don't want to make the loan for that reason.

73
00:43:19,800 --> 00:43:21,120
All right.

74
00:43:21,120 --> 00:43:24,000
So any time that there is an issue with collateral,

75
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:27,840
then this can explain what's going on.

76
00:43:27,840 --> 00:43:31,600
It's a good story for a lot of cases.

77
00:43:31,600 --> 00:43:35,560
But the only cases where these work are, of course,

78
00:43:35,560 --> 00:43:37,840
ones where people are genuinely liquidity constrained,

79
00:43:37,840 --> 00:43:40,840
when it is a person without collateral who's trying to just

80
00:43:40,840 --> 00:43:44,000
get an unsecured cash loan.

81
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:46,640
But once you've got decent collateral,

82
00:43:46,640 --> 00:43:49,480
and again, the most obvious one in the modern economy

83
00:43:49,480 --> 00:43:50,960
is home equity.

84
00:43:50,960 --> 00:43:53,760
Home equity is some of the best collateral around.

85
00:43:53,760 --> 00:43:56,920
You can use it to get really low interest loans, which

86
00:43:56,920 --> 00:43:59,240
are even better than they seem because you can deduct all

87
00:43:59,240 --> 00:44:00,800
that interest.

88
00:44:00,800 --> 00:44:02,320
There's a little issue.

89
00:44:02,320 --> 00:44:03,800
Technically, sometimes the interest

90
00:44:03,800 --> 00:44:05,200
is only tax deductible if you say

91
00:44:05,200 --> 00:44:07,280
it's being used for home improvement.

92
00:44:07,280 --> 00:44:09,560
But how well does the IRS monitor

93
00:44:09,560 --> 00:44:11,960
whether you're really using your loans for home improvement

94
00:44:11,960 --> 00:44:15,120
or you say you're using them for home improvement?

95
00:44:15,120 --> 00:44:17,720
I've never heard of any monitoring of any kind.

96
00:44:17,720 --> 00:44:19,880
