01:07:43,160 --> 01:07:47,760
And I'm always thinking, well, you're going and complaining about people that know a lot

151
01:07:47,760 --> 01:07:48,760
who should know a bit more.

152
01:07:48,760 --> 01:07:53,160
But you've got to remember, there's like a giant 95% of people who don't even understand

153
01:07:53,160 --> 01:07:54,160
this thing.

154
01:07:54,160 --> 01:07:57,160
And you don't seem to talk about those people at all.

155
01:07:57,160 --> 01:08:02,160
So before you get really mad at people who understand this but take it too far, you might

156
01:08:02,160 --> 01:08:05,160
want to remember there's a bunch of people who don't even know that.

157
01:08:05,160 --> 01:08:09,160
But I didn't want to get very mad at you.

158
01:08:09,160 --> 01:08:13,160
Plus, I had better things to talk about than Nassim Taleb.

159
01:08:13,160 --> 01:08:14,160
All right.

160
01:08:14,160 --> 01:08:15,160
So questions?

161
01:08:15,160 --> 01:08:16,160
Questions?

162
01:08:16,160 --> 01:08:18,160
I'm happy.

163
01:08:18,160 --> 01:08:19,160
All right.

164
01:08:19,160 --> 01:08:20,160
So now mean variance efficiency.

165
01:08:20,160 --> 01:08:21,160
All right.

166
01:08:21,160 --> 01:08:23,160
Now, a popular simplifying assumption.

167
01:08:23,160 --> 01:08:26,160
So it doesn't mean it's true, but popular and it's in it.

168
01:08:26,160 --> 01:08:27,160
And it's simplifying.

169
01:08:27,160 --> 01:08:28,160
People use it in finance.

170
01:08:28,160 --> 01:08:30,160
Say people care about only two things.

171
01:08:30,160 --> 01:08:35,160
They care about the meaning of consumption and they care about the variance of consumption.

172
01:08:35,160 --> 01:08:36,160
All right.

173
01:08:36,160 --> 01:08:40,160
So they don't care about, say, the kurtosis of consumption or anything like that.

174
01:08:40,160 --> 01:08:41,160
All right.

175
01:08:41,160 --> 01:08:45,160
So the idea is that the higher the mean and the lower the variance, the higher utility.

176
01:08:45,160 --> 01:08:50,160
So utility goes up when the mean goes up and utility goes up when the variance goes down.

177
01:08:50,160 --> 01:08:51,160
All right.

178
01:08:51,160 --> 01:08:58,160
And what this means is that people are going to be selecting portfolios that are on a mean

179
01:08:58,160 --> 01:08:59,160
variance budget constraint.

180
01:08:59,160 --> 01:09:00,160
All right.

181
01:09:00,160 --> 01:09:07,160
So if this is mean, this is variance, then the only way you're going to take it other

182
01:09:07,160 --> 01:09:08,160
than to be indifferent.

183
01:09:08,160 --> 01:09:10,160
So you think about a point of indifference.

184
01:09:10,160 --> 01:09:12,160
It could be something like this.

185
01:09:12,160 --> 01:09:16,160
Where if mean goes up and the variance goes up, you could be indifferent.

186
01:09:16,160 --> 01:09:20,160
So start off like moderate mean, moderate variance, high mean, high variance.

187
01:09:20,160 --> 01:09:23,160
So all of these are possibilities for your indifference.

188
01:09:23,160 --> 01:09:27,160
And then you always want to be on one of the frontier at least.

189
01:09:27,160 --> 01:09:32,160
There's never a reason to go and say, go to here.

190
01:09:32,160 --> 01:09:34,160
There's no reason to be there.

191
01:09:34,160 --> 01:09:39,160
So look, if you're going to be at that level of variance, why not go out to that high mean?

192
01:09:39,160 --> 01:09:43,160
Or at least, if you're going to be that mean, why not go down to that low variance?

193
01:09:43,160 --> 01:09:46,160
All right.

194
01:09:46,160 --> 01:09:51,160
Now, if you work through the assumptions of a model where people only care about mean

195
01:09:51,160 --> 01:09:57,160
and variance, then you get a classic equation in finance.

196
01:09:57,160 --> 01:10:01,160
And the equation is this.

197
01:10:01,160 --> 01:10:15,160
R bar A is equal to R naught plus sigma E over sigma E times R E bar minus R naught.

198
01:10:15,160 --> 01:10:17,160
Parenthesis, this can be proven.

199
01:10:17,160 --> 01:10:18,160
I'm not going to prove it.

200
01:10:18,160 --> 01:10:19,160
You won't have to prove it.

201
01:10:19,160 --> 01:10:21,160
It's in the next book.

202
01:10:21,160 --> 01:10:25,160
All right, now, the translation makes a lot more sense than the equation probably.

203
01:10:25,160 --> 01:10:30,160
So left hand side, the expected return on asset A.

204
01:10:30,160 --> 01:10:31,160
It's all bar A.

205
01:10:31,160 --> 01:10:34,160
The expected return on asset A is equal to the risk-free rate.

206
01:10:34,160 --> 01:10:38,160
So R naught is the risk-free rate, which when people do empirical finance, usually it is

207
01:10:38,160 --> 01:10:41,160
put in the 30-year treasury bill or something like that.

208
01:10:41,160 --> 01:10:45,160
So that's, of course, you might say, that's not really certain, is it?

209
01:10:45,160 --> 01:10:47,160
Some countries default.

210
01:10:47,160 --> 01:10:51,160
But usually in practice, people say, oh, it's probably narrow.

211
01:10:51,160 --> 01:10:52,160
I don't know.

212
01:10:52,160 --> 01:10:53,160
Let's just figure.

213
01:10:53,160 --> 01:10:59,160
Of course, if you're in Greece, then you might say, well, it's the EU debt, not Greek debt.

214
01:10:59,160 --> 01:11:01,160
Which is risk-free.

215
01:11:01,160 --> 01:11:07,160
All right, so equals the risk-free rate plus the difference.

216
01:11:07,160 --> 01:11:12,160
Let's see, plus the difference between the average market rate, which is R bar E, and

217
01:11:12,160 --> 01:11:13,160
the risk-free rate.

218
01:11:13,160 --> 01:11:15,160
So that's what those terms represent.

219
01:11:15,160 --> 01:11:18,160
So that is the expected rate of return on the whole market.

220
01:11:18,160 --> 01:11:20,160
And that's, again, the risk-free rate.

221
01:11:20,160 --> 01:11:26,160
And then times the ratio of the covariance of A's return on the average market rate

222
01:11:26,160 --> 01:11:30,160
to the variance of the average market return.

223
01:11:30,160 --> 01:11:37,160
Now, this thing here, you might recognize that as a regression coefficient.

224
01:11:37,160 --> 01:11:40,160
Remember the formula for regression coefficients?

225
01:11:40,160 --> 01:11:42,160
So covariance over variance.

226
01:11:42,160 --> 01:11:43,160
Remember that one?

227
01:11:43,160 --> 01:11:44,160
Remember this?

228
01:11:44,160 --> 01:11:45,160
All right.

229
01:11:45,160 --> 01:11:49,160
That's actually the coefficient you would get if you regress asset A's return on the

230
01:11:49,160 --> 01:11:50,160
average market return.

231
01:11:50,160 --> 01:11:55,160
So just imagine we get a data set where we get the return on one asset.

232
01:11:55,160 --> 01:11:59,160
And then we also get the return on whatever stock index you like.

233
01:11:59,160 --> 01:12:01,160
And then you just go and do that regression.

234
01:12:01,160 --> 01:12:04,160
And that thing is the coefficient you'll get.

235
01:12:04,160 --> 01:12:10,160
And remember, what Greek letter do we usually give for the regression coefficient?

236
01:12:10,160 --> 01:12:11,160
Beta.

237
01:12:11,160 --> 01:12:12,160
Beta.

238
01:12:12,160 --> 01:12:13,160
Yeah, beta.

239
01:12:13,160 --> 01:12:14,160
Right?

240
01:12:14,160 --> 01:12:16,160
And that's why this is called the beta of the asset.

241
01:12:16,160 --> 01:12:19,160
Have you ever heard, you know, the asset's beta?

242
01:12:19,160 --> 01:12:20,160
Yes, it's beta.

243
01:12:20,160 --> 01:12:24,160
That is the regression coefficient of that asset's return on the overall market rate

244
01:12:24,160 --> 01:12:25,160
return.

245
01:12:25,160 --> 01:12:32,160
Yeah, so you know, like you got a beta of.8, that says that when the market goes up

246
01:12:32,160 --> 01:12:34,160
by one, this thing goes up by.8.

247
01:12:34,160 --> 01:12:35,160
Make sense?

248
01:12:35,160 --> 01:12:36,160
Right?

249
01:12:36,160 --> 01:12:40,160
And then of course, if beta were zero, it would indicate that the thing had no correlation

250
01:12:40,160 --> 01:12:42,160
at all with the market rate return.

251
01:12:42,160 --> 01:12:43,160
Right?

252
01:12:43,160 --> 01:12:45,160
And that works.

253
01:12:45,160 --> 01:12:46,160
Yeah?

254
01:12:46,160 --> 01:12:47,160
Okay.

255
01:12:47,160 --> 01:12:48,160
All right.

256
01:12:48,160 --> 01:12:51,160
So questions about formula?

257
01:12:51,160 --> 01:12:58,160
So, again, it's a little complicated, but makes intuitive sense once you break the piece

258
01:12:58,160 --> 01:12:59,160
of art.

259
01:12:59,160 --> 01:13:03,160
And proving it's art, but we're not going to break it.

260
01:13:03,160 --> 01:13:05,160
So, here we go.

