269
01:13:21,160 --> 01:13:30,160
Now, moving on to something else that bothers my dad, the efficient markets hypothesis.

270
01:13:30,160 --> 01:13:32,160
All right.

271
01:13:32,160 --> 01:13:37,160
Now, once you've got this formula for return on assets, it's pretty obvious what's got

272
01:13:37,160 --> 01:13:41,160
to happen when new information comes along, which is that market prices have to adjust.

273
01:13:41,160 --> 01:13:46,160
So they rise if there's good news, and they fall if there's bad news.

274
01:13:46,160 --> 01:13:47,160
Why?

275
01:13:47,160 --> 01:13:49,160
Because otherwise the return equation wouldn't be satisfied.

276
01:13:49,160 --> 01:13:53,160
So if you got some good news and prices didn't change, this would mean that you're paying

277
01:13:53,160 --> 01:13:56,160
the same amount for an asset that's expected to be better.

278
01:13:56,160 --> 01:13:57,160
Right?

279
01:13:57,160 --> 01:14:01,160
And if you can expect an asset to do better than originally, this means that it's earning

280
01:14:01,160 --> 01:14:02,160
a higher return.

281
01:14:02,160 --> 01:14:03,160
All right?

282
01:14:03,160 --> 01:14:07,160
So it makes sense that when new news comes in, that prices will go up.

283
01:14:07,160 --> 01:14:09,160
When good news comes in, prices go up.

284
01:14:09,160 --> 01:14:11,160
When bad news comes in, prices go down.

285
01:14:11,160 --> 01:14:17,160
Even if the good or bad events are far in the future, as long as you can see them coming,

286
01:14:17,160 --> 01:14:21,160
or as long as the probabilities even have altered, that's enough.

287
01:14:21,160 --> 01:14:22,160
All right.

288
01:14:22,160 --> 01:14:28,160
Now, this seems super obvious until you reflect deeply on what it means for information to

289
01:14:28,160 --> 01:14:29,160
arrive.

290
01:14:29,160 --> 01:14:31,160
When does information arrive?

291
01:14:31,160 --> 01:14:33,160
When does it arrive?

292
01:14:33,160 --> 01:14:35,160
The answer is, oh, sorry.

293
01:14:35,160 --> 01:14:37,160
I thought there was a hand going up.

294
01:14:37,160 --> 01:14:39,160
So when does information arrive?

295
01:14:39,160 --> 01:14:41,160
Like, here's when information arrives.

296
01:14:41,160 --> 01:14:42,160
Yes, now.

297
01:14:42,160 --> 01:14:43,160
All right.

298
01:14:43,160 --> 01:14:44,160
Information arrives.

299
01:14:44,160 --> 01:14:46,160
So we like, anyway.

300
01:14:46,160 --> 01:14:50,160
Information often arrives long before anything changes.

301
01:14:50,160 --> 01:14:54,160
And on the other hand, very often if you look at the front page of the newspaper, there's

302
01:14:54,160 --> 01:14:56,160
no news in the newspaper.

303
01:14:56,160 --> 01:15:01,160
Like, if you go and open up the newspaper, like, no Israeli-Palestinian deal reached

304
01:15:01,160 --> 01:15:02,160
again.

305
01:15:02,160 --> 01:15:05,160
Well, that's just what I expected would occur.

306
01:15:05,160 --> 01:15:07,160
So the news is not news.

307
01:15:07,160 --> 01:15:08,160
All right?

308
01:15:08,160 --> 01:15:10,160
It needed to be true news.

309
01:15:10,160 --> 01:15:13,160
It would not have to, it doesn't mean that it appears in a newspaper.

310
01:15:13,160 --> 01:15:17,160
It would have to be something different from what was foreseeable before.

311
01:15:17,160 --> 01:15:19,160
All right.

312
01:15:19,160 --> 01:15:23,160
So like, you find out a firm is going to have to pay a million dollars ten years from now.

313
01:15:23,160 --> 01:15:25,160
The price has to fall now.

314
01:15:25,160 --> 01:15:28,160
You don't wait around for the actual bill to come.

315
01:15:28,160 --> 01:15:29,160
The bill is not the news.

316
01:15:29,160 --> 01:15:31,160
You know, it's the legal ruling.

317
01:15:31,160 --> 01:15:36,160
Or maybe it's the judgment of legal experts that a ruling is likely to come out a certain

318
01:15:36,160 --> 01:15:37,160
way.

319
01:15:37,160 --> 01:15:42,160
Or maybe it's the judgment of the forensic accountants that when people find out about

320
01:15:42,160 --> 01:15:46,160
what's going to happen with the numbers, that then the legal experts will say something.

321
01:15:46,160 --> 01:15:47,160
All right.

322
01:15:47,160 --> 01:15:51,160
And of course, this applies totally to probabilistic news.

323
01:15:51,160 --> 01:15:55,160
So if there's like a 30% chance of a hurricane coming and then it goes to 40, it's a reason

324
01:15:55,160 --> 01:15:58,160
for prices to go and change right away.

325
01:15:58,160 --> 01:15:59,160
Right?

326
01:15:59,160 --> 01:16:01,160
Even though the hurricane hasn't even begun yet.

327
01:16:01,160 --> 01:16:05,160
But say, well, just the odds of a hurricane are bigger and that's going to change the

328
01:16:05,160 --> 01:16:07,160
prices of everything.

329
01:16:07,160 --> 01:16:08,160
All right.

330
01:16:08,160 --> 01:16:11,160
Now, of course, this means the occurrence of any expected pattern is not news.

331
01:16:11,160 --> 01:16:15,160
So when you think about how many headlines are just like, duh, of course.

332
01:16:15,160 --> 01:16:17,160
Like, how is that even news?

333
01:16:17,160 --> 01:16:18,160
Right?

334
01:16:18,160 --> 01:16:22,160
So, like, although of course it gets even worse for headlines saying that it's not.

335
01:16:22,160 --> 01:16:23,160
Right?

336
01:16:23,160 --> 01:16:24,160
But anyway.

337
01:16:24,160 --> 01:16:30,160
But normally, there's a lot of so-called news where it's like, you know, Trump gets nomination

338
01:16:30,160 --> 01:16:34,160
when a day before, like, he had all the votes, everything was lined up.

339
01:16:34,160 --> 01:16:38,160
Journalists will still consider this news, but economically it's not really news.

340
01:16:38,160 --> 01:16:39,160
Right?

341
01:16:39,160 --> 01:16:43,160
Anyway, of course, you might say, well, there's always a little bit of news.

342
01:16:43,160 --> 01:16:46,160
Because there was always a chance that he could die of a heart attack.

343
01:16:46,160 --> 01:16:47,160
All right.

344
01:16:47,160 --> 01:16:48,160
All right.

345
01:16:48,160 --> 01:16:49,160
Fine.

346
01:16:49,160 --> 01:16:50,160
All right.

347
01:16:50,160 --> 01:16:52,160
But then what really should be reflected in the market isn't that he got the nomination,

348
01:16:52,160 --> 01:16:56,160
which is already well known, but that he didn't die of a heart attack, which was a very small

349
01:16:56,160 --> 01:16:58,160
lingering probability.

350
01:16:58,160 --> 01:17:00,160
It was always there.

351
01:17:00,160 --> 01:17:01,160
Right?

352
01:17:01,160 --> 01:17:02,160
But still not much.

353
01:17:02,160 --> 01:17:03,160
Yeah.

354
01:17:03,160 --> 01:17:04,160
So, like, what's the audience?

355
01:17:04,160 --> 01:17:09,160
Like, if somebody operates under the probability that something will happen versus somebody

356
01:17:09,160 --> 01:17:11,160
operating that it won't happen.

357
01:17:11,160 --> 01:17:12,160
Right.

358
01:17:12,160 --> 01:17:13,160
Right.

359
01:17:13,160 --> 01:17:15,160
And the official answer to that is it all depends on the information held by the marginal

360
01:17:15,160 --> 01:17:16,160
trader.

361
01:17:16,160 --> 01:17:19,160
So, the marginal trader and what he knows is what's crucial.

362
01:17:19,160 --> 01:17:20,160
Yes.

363
01:17:20,160 --> 01:17:23,160
In fact, there's some kindergarteners who don't know where everything is news to them.

364
01:17:23,160 --> 01:17:26,160
Like, whatever you say is like, what?

365
01:17:26,160 --> 01:17:30,160
Or, of course, they might not even understand what the claim is.

366
01:17:30,160 --> 01:17:31,160
They don't matter.

367
01:17:31,160 --> 01:17:33,160
They're not buying or selling assets anyway.

368
01:17:33,160 --> 01:17:36,160
It's the marginal trader, the marginal person, and what that person knows that's actually

369
01:17:36,160 --> 01:17:37,160
crucial.

370
01:17:37,160 --> 01:17:38,160
All right.

371
01:17:38,160 --> 01:17:43,160
Now, there's a surprising implication out of all this, and that is that asset changes

372
01:17:43,160 --> 01:17:45,160
should be totally unpredictable.

373
01:17:45,160 --> 01:17:50,160
Asset changes should be totally unpredictable, or technically they should follow a random

374
01:17:50,160 --> 01:17:57,160
walk, such as expected value, expected price at time t plus 1 equals the price at time

375
01:17:57,160 --> 01:17:58,160
t.

376
01:17:58,160 --> 01:18:04,160
And equations like this, this is known as the efficient markets hypothesis, EMH.

377
01:18:04,160 --> 01:18:06,160
All right.

378
01:18:06,160 --> 01:18:07,160
Okay.

379
01:18:07,160 --> 01:18:13,160
So, EMH was widely denounced during the last recession, and of course was widely denounced

380
01:18:13,160 --> 01:18:15,160
before that as well.

381
01:18:15,160 --> 01:18:20,160
But there's two kinds of denouncers of the efficient markets hypothesis.

382
01:18:20,160 --> 01:18:24,160
There's the denouncers who just say, it's complete total garbage.

383
01:18:24,160 --> 01:18:26,160
There's nothing to it.

384
01:18:26,160 --> 01:18:29,160
And they don't know what they're talking about.

385
01:18:29,160 --> 01:18:34,160
And then there are the actual quants who criticize it, where if you strip away the adjectives

