01:29:38,600 --> 01:29:39,960
All right.

229
01:29:39,960 --> 01:29:42,120
And then as I said, when you look at net returns,

230
01:29:42,120 --> 01:29:45,520
that is even more weird that index funds are not going,

231
01:29:45,520 --> 01:29:47,200
are not even bigger than they are.

232
01:29:47,200 --> 01:29:48,360
Because the asserts theory suggests

233
01:29:48,360 --> 01:29:50,520
that it should be the net returns that equalize,

234
01:29:50,520 --> 01:29:52,880
but so far, it looks more like the gross returns

235
01:29:52,880 --> 01:29:54,560
that equalize.

236
01:29:54,560 --> 01:29:56,640
Right now, many people pointed out quite properly

237
01:29:56,640 --> 01:30:00,520
that if everyone did indexing, it wouldn't work anymore.

238
01:30:00,520 --> 01:30:03,400
But we haven't gotten there yet.

239
01:30:03,400 --> 01:30:05,000
Indexing had been around for 50 years,

240
01:30:05,000 --> 01:30:10,200
and we're still slowly moving towards final equilibrium.

241
01:30:10,200 --> 01:30:13,040
Like once it was 99% of people indexing,

242
01:30:13,040 --> 01:30:15,240
then the few people that were around who would say,

243
01:30:15,240 --> 01:30:16,400
you know what?

244
01:30:16,400 --> 01:30:17,840
The hurricane's coming to Florida,

245
01:30:17,840 --> 01:30:22,120
so I'm going to go and buy orange futures, which

246
01:30:22,120 --> 01:30:23,240
no one else would be doing.

247
01:30:23,240 --> 01:30:25,360
All the indexers would just be ignoring the weather.

248
01:30:25,360 --> 01:30:27,240
So there'd be a few people who would continue

249
01:30:27,240 --> 01:30:29,280
to be aware of the world who could then

250
01:30:29,280 --> 01:30:32,920
have very low-hanging fruit, very easy ways of making money.

251
01:30:32,920 --> 01:30:34,640
But we're not there yet.

252
01:30:34,640 --> 01:30:35,920
We don't see this even close.

253
01:30:35,920 --> 01:30:38,760
We don't have any concrete world of this.

254
01:30:38,760 --> 01:30:40,360
All right, questions?

255
01:30:40,360 --> 01:30:41,320
Everyone happy?

256
01:30:41,320 --> 01:30:43,120
Everyone indexing already?

257
01:30:43,120 --> 01:30:44,480
All right.

258
01:30:44,480 --> 01:30:45,480
Yeah.

259
01:30:45,480 --> 01:30:46,880
All right.

260
01:30:46,880 --> 01:30:51,040
So despite the logic of the AMH, there

261
01:30:51,040 --> 01:30:53,680
are some noted anomalies.

262
01:30:53,680 --> 01:30:55,120
All right.

263
01:30:55,120 --> 01:30:59,000
Some of the best publicized are so-called calendar effects.

264
01:30:59,000 --> 01:31:07,320
And the best known one is the January effect, which

265
01:31:07,320 --> 01:31:08,880
at least used to be super solid.

266
01:31:08,880 --> 01:31:13,840
Now, the least in common view is the January effect

267
01:31:13,840 --> 01:31:14,760
has finally gone away.

268
01:31:14,760 --> 01:31:16,320
But it still lasted for decades.

269
01:31:16,320 --> 01:31:19,240
So it's still worth talking about, even if it's gone

270
01:31:19,240 --> 01:31:21,440
and doesn't help you anymore.

271
01:31:21,440 --> 01:31:23,680
So Thaler goes over the evidence at the time.

272
01:31:23,680 --> 01:31:24,440
Yeah, Jonathan?

273
01:31:24,440 --> 01:31:26,520
Do you think part of the reason that it went away

274
01:31:26,520 --> 01:31:28,360
is that this was published 35 years ago now?

275
01:31:28,360 --> 01:31:28,760
Yeah.

276
01:31:28,760 --> 01:31:29,260
Yeah, yeah.

277
01:31:29,260 --> 01:31:31,200
So something that people would say,

278
01:31:31,200 --> 01:31:33,720
the industry would sometimes say is now the January effect's

279
01:31:33,720 --> 01:31:36,560
happening in December or November.

280
01:31:36,560 --> 01:31:39,400
And then eventually, it's like, well, we can't find it anymore.

281
01:31:39,400 --> 01:31:42,600
But yeah, so that's pretty simple.

282
01:31:42,600 --> 01:31:43,080
All right.

283
01:31:43,080 --> 01:31:46,280
So anyway, so for the period that Richard Thaler

284
01:31:46,280 --> 01:31:49,240
is talking about, so you've got an average New York Stock

285
01:31:49,240 --> 01:31:51,920
Exchange monthly returns from February, December, 0.5%

286
01:31:51,920 --> 01:31:54,880
per month, and for January, 3.5.

287
01:31:54,880 --> 01:31:56,840
So seven times the average for one month.

288
01:31:56,840 --> 01:31:58,360
That seems pretty good.

289
01:31:58,360 --> 01:32:01,640
And looking more closely, seems like it's primarily

290
01:32:01,640 --> 01:32:06,040
just from really high returns for small firms during January.

291
01:32:06,040 --> 01:32:10,480
So it's a subset of the one month, one subset of firms

292
01:32:10,480 --> 01:32:13,080
driving this enormous return.

293
01:32:13,080 --> 01:32:15,520
And if it were just one country, it'd be easy to dismiss.

294
01:32:15,520 --> 01:32:17,840
But so at least what Thaler's writing,

295
01:32:17,840 --> 01:32:21,200
January effect's found in 15 out of 16 countries studied.

296
01:32:21,200 --> 01:32:24,600
So again, that's pretty impressive.

297
01:32:24,600 --> 01:32:26,920
So that's the big one that's been talked about.

298
01:32:26,920 --> 01:32:29,840
And again, now, it may no longer exist.

299
01:32:29,840 --> 01:32:32,840
But the fact that it was around for decades still gives one

300
01:32:32,840 --> 01:32:33,560
pause.

301
01:32:33,560 --> 01:32:36,280
What's the explanation for it?

302
01:32:36,280 --> 01:32:38,240
So good question.

303
01:32:38,240 --> 01:32:40,160
So if you read Thaler, and I believe

304
01:32:40,160 --> 01:32:43,800
the chapter is assigned for the final exam and everything,

305
01:32:43,800 --> 01:32:47,200
one popular story is something to do with tax law.

306
01:32:47,200 --> 01:32:51,600
And a lot of people just want to go and sell stuff

307
01:32:51,600 --> 01:32:55,640
right before, either right before or right after.

308
01:32:55,640 --> 01:32:57,440
But again, I think what he said is actually

309
01:32:57,440 --> 01:33:00,120
if you go and look at countries where they do or don't

310
01:33:00,120 --> 01:33:02,200
have these tax incentives, you didn't actually

311
01:33:02,200 --> 01:33:03,800
see much of a difference.

312
01:33:03,800 --> 01:33:05,240
So that's wrong.

313
01:33:05,240 --> 01:33:08,240
So then it's pretty hard to know.

314
01:33:08,240 --> 01:33:10,480
My memory is Thaler doesn't have any good story.

315
01:33:10,480 --> 01:33:12,080
It's like, well, you could either

316
01:33:12,080 --> 01:33:14,080
sit around saying I won't believe it until you

317
01:33:14,080 --> 01:33:16,580
have a good story, or you could just make some money going

318
01:33:16,580 --> 01:33:17,760
and buying in January.

319
01:33:17,760 --> 01:33:22,200
And it worked pretty well for a couple decades.

320
01:33:22,200 --> 01:33:23,320
All right.

321
01:33:23,320 --> 01:33:31,320
Now another calendar effect, which again is not as

322
01:33:31,320 --> 01:33:33,520
devastating and harder to actually make money on it,

323
01:33:33,520 --> 01:33:35,040
but it's called the weekend effect.

324
01:33:35,040 --> 01:33:38,880
So when you go and leave money in the bank

325
01:33:38,880 --> 01:33:41,880
over the weekend for three days, how many days worth of interest

326
01:33:41,880 --> 01:33:42,840
do you earn?

327
01:33:42,840 --> 01:33:43,360
Three.

328
01:33:43,360 --> 01:33:44,600
Three days.

329
01:33:44,600 --> 01:33:48,600
When you go and when the stock market closes over the weekend,

330
01:33:48,600 --> 01:33:50,960
how much of an extra return do you get on Monday

331
01:33:50,960 --> 01:33:53,160
when it opens up?

332
01:33:53,160 --> 01:33:55,520
It seems like you only get one day instead of three days.

333
01:33:55,520 --> 01:33:57,320
And that seems weird.

334
01:33:57,320 --> 01:33:59,800
So if you hold debt, you get three days worth of interest

335
01:33:59,800 --> 01:34:00,960
over the weekend.

336
01:34:00,960 --> 01:34:04,200
So why don't you get the same for stocks?

337
01:34:04,200 --> 01:34:06,520
But anyway, Monday returns don't seem especially high.

338
01:34:06,520 --> 01:34:07,520
And again, that's weird.

339
01:34:07,520 --> 01:34:10,600
You think there'd be arbitrage, like why not go and dump all?

340
01:34:10,600 --> 01:34:15,320
So since debt pays triple over the weekend and stocks don't,

341
01:34:15,320 --> 01:34:17,600
why not dump all your stocks right

342
01:34:17,600 --> 01:34:19,760
before the end of the day Friday, put it all in bonds.

343
01:34:19,760 --> 01:34:22,220
And then every morning, you get out of that extra interest,

344
01:34:22,220 --> 01:34:23,480
buy stocks.

345
01:34:23,480 --> 01:34:25,820
All right, now there is the problem of transactions costs,

346
01:34:25,820 --> 01:34:27,680
which Thaler is happy to admit.

347
01:34:27,680 --> 01:34:29,440
Even there, though, they're at the margin.

348
01:34:29,440 --> 01:34:33,600
If you were planning on selling, why not arrange all your selling

349
01:34:33,600 --> 01:34:35,520
to be at the end of the day of stocks,

350
01:34:35,520 --> 01:34:37,560
sell all at the end of the day on February,

351
01:34:37,560 --> 01:34:41,560
arrange all of your buying to be at the opening on Monday?

352
01:34:41,560 --> 01:34:44,960
So that would seem like a good idea.

353
01:34:44,960 --> 01:34:49,000
So Thaler, being one of the reasonable critics of EMH,

354
01:34:49,000 --> 01:34:52,160
is willing to admit cases where transactions costs mess up

355
01:34:52,160 --> 01:34:54,200
your opportunity to make money.

356
01:34:54,200 --> 01:34:55,720
All right, but again, like he says,

357
01:34:55,720 --> 01:34:59,240
well, look, still, we should see people going and timing

358
01:34:59,240 --> 01:35:02,720
all of their sales and purchases around these things.

359
01:35:02,720 --> 01:35:05,240
So basically, if you know that you're going to sell,

360
01:35:05,240 --> 01:35:07,880
why not sell at the minute where it's going to be best for you?

361
01:35:07,880 --> 01:35:09,160
If you know where you're going to buy,

362
01:35:09,160 --> 01:35:11,660
why not buy at the minutes that are going to be best for you?

363
01:35:11,660 --> 01:35:14,880
And yet, it seems like very few people actually do this.

364
01:35:18,720 --> 01:35:20,440
All right, so let's see.

365
01:35:20,440 --> 01:35:24,920
And then there's actually a series of papers on,

366
01:35:24,920 --> 01:35:26,080
what is it?

367
01:35:26,080 --> 01:35:29,000
So stock markets and seasonal affective disorder.

368
01:35:29,000 --> 01:35:31,080
So at least there's a paper in the ER

369
01:35:31,080 --> 01:35:34,640
saying that you can actually see that stock markets

370
01:35:34,640 --> 01:35:40,040
around the world, so basically, like, so let's see.

371
01:35:40,040 --> 01:35:44,880
So basically, stock prices go up in the northern hemisphere

372
01:35:44,880 --> 01:35:49,640
during the summer and go down during May through July

373
01:35:49,640 --> 01:35:51,480
and go down in the southern hemisphere.

374
01:35:51,480 --> 01:35:53,060
And actually claiming that even if you

375
01:35:53,060 --> 01:35:54,720
look at the city of the stock market,

376
01:35:54,720 --> 01:35:56,480
that the amount of sunlight they're getting

377
01:35:56,480 --> 01:36:00,520
seems to be affecting the price, which on the one hand,

378
01:36:00,520 --> 01:36:02,600
is a little hard to believe because you think,

379
01:36:02,600 --> 01:36:04,760
like, arbitrage should handle this.

380
01:36:04,760 --> 01:36:07,720
But the pattern is actually pretty interesting.

381
01:36:07,720 --> 01:36:09,440
I did meet one of the authors, and she

382
01:36:09,440 --> 01:36:12,640
seemed to have a lot of details.

383
01:36:12,640 --> 01:36:14,760
I haven't talked to anyone who disagrees with you,

384
01:36:14,760 --> 01:36:16,880
but you've got a pretty good story anyway.

385
01:36:16,880 --> 01:36:19,880
I seem to pass a bunch of surprising tests, like the

386
01:36:19,880 --> 01:36:22,520
closer you are to the equator, the lower this is,

387
01:36:22,520 --> 01:36:24,720
because then the less of a change in daylight

388
01:36:24,720 --> 01:36:25,440
you're getting.

389
01:36:25,440 --> 01:36:26,900
Because seasonal affective disorder

390
01:36:26,900 --> 01:36:29,360
is all about people getting depressed by too much night.

391
01:36:29,360 --> 01:36:30,900
Could be too depressed to arbitrage.

392
01:36:30,900 --> 01:36:32,280
Yes, too depressed to arbitrage.

393
01:36:32,280 --> 01:36:36,000
That astrology thing wasn't so crazy after all.

394
01:36:36,000 --> 01:36:38,960
Well, the constant problem with astrology

395
01:36:38,960 --> 01:36:41,320
is there isn't even a standard astrological prediction.

396
01:36:41,320 --> 01:36:43,360
Seasonal affective disorder does have predictions

397
01:36:43,360 --> 01:36:46,240
that are specific and so testable.

398
01:36:46,240 --> 01:36:49,520
All right, now we come to one of my favorite ones,

399
01:36:49,520 --> 01:36:50,960
a mean reversion.

400
01:36:50,960 --> 01:36:54,480
So Fisher-Marcus hypothesis says that returns are unpredictable.

401
01:36:54,480 --> 01:36:57,320
You can't use past returns to forecast future.

402
01:36:57,320 --> 01:37:00,000
But there is a literature on mean reversion

403
01:37:00,000 --> 01:37:02,960
saying that that is too hasty, and at least

404
01:37:02,960 --> 01:37:05,800
for certain assets.

405
01:37:05,800 --> 01:37:08,160
The fact that rates have been the thing,

406
01:37:08,160 --> 01:37:10,240
the market's been going up a lot,

407
01:37:10,240 --> 01:37:12,400
means that you should predict it will go down.

408
01:37:12,400 --> 01:37:13,780
And the fact it's been going down

409
01:37:13,780 --> 01:37:15,200
means it should go up.

410
01:37:15,200 --> 01:37:17,080
Which, notice, is basically the exact opposite

411
01:37:17,080 --> 01:37:18,400
of the normal theory.

412
01:37:18,400 --> 01:37:20,920
The normal theory is like momentum.

413
01:37:20,920 --> 01:37:23,240
Good things follow good, bad things follow bad.

414
01:37:23,240 --> 01:37:25,760
Mean reversion is the opposite.

415
01:37:25,760 --> 01:37:28,360
It says that you know how everything's been going great?

416
01:37:28,360 --> 01:37:29,880
Expect bad stuff to happen.

417
01:37:29,880 --> 01:37:31,680
You know how everything's been going poorly?

418
01:37:31,680 --> 01:37:34,160
Expect good things to happen.

419
01:37:34,160 --> 01:37:35,600
All right, yes?

420
01:37:35,600 --> 01:37:39,120
Just sort of clarify, though, with mean reversion,

421
01:37:39,120 --> 01:37:42,040
would it then, like if something goes up by a certain amount,

422
01:37:42,040 --> 01:37:44,400
does it have to drop by that same exact amount?

423
01:37:44,400 --> 01:37:45,600
Or just a significant amount?

424
01:37:45,600 --> 01:37:46,100
Right.

425
01:37:46,100 --> 01:37:48,260
Again, the way that you normally do it statistically

426
01:37:48,260 --> 01:37:51,400
is just to see whether there is a statistically significant

427
01:37:51,400 --> 01:37:53,120
tendency for higher average returns

428
01:37:53,120 --> 01:37:56,640
in the past to actually have a negative sign.

429
01:37:56,640 --> 01:37:59,880
But yeah, so it doesn't actually require that you

430
01:37:59,880 --> 01:38:02,800
go back to a specific mean.

431
01:38:02,800 --> 01:38:05,280
It just means that when you're above a historic mean,

432
01:38:05,280 --> 01:38:06,880
you tend to go down.

433
01:38:06,880 --> 01:38:09,600
And but again, like tendency can be quite slow.

434
01:38:09,600 --> 01:38:13,400
So mean reversion is one of my favorites.

435
01:38:13,400 --> 01:38:18,720
So when I was betting Tyler Cowen on the price of gasoline,

436
01:38:18,720 --> 01:38:20,160
mean reversion was in my mind.

437
01:38:20,160 --> 01:38:21,360
That's all I knew.

438
01:38:21,360 --> 01:38:24,360
So I talked to James Hamilton, by many accounts,

439
01:38:24,360 --> 01:38:26,880
the best empirical energy economist in the world.

440
01:38:26,880 --> 01:38:29,680
And he totally believed in mean reversion.

441
01:38:29,680 --> 01:38:32,200
And the argument got started because Tyler Cowen said

442
01:38:32,200 --> 01:38:34,400
Julian Simon didn't know what he was talking about,

443
01:38:34,400 --> 01:38:36,960
because the EMH rules out the idea

444
01:38:36,960 --> 01:38:39,800
that natural resource prices tend to go back down

445
01:38:39,800 --> 01:38:40,840
when they're high.

446
01:38:40,840 --> 01:38:43,840
And I said, you know, Simon had 150 years of data on his side,

447
01:38:43,840 --> 01:38:45,320
and you've got a theory.

448
01:38:45,320 --> 01:38:48,560
So I'm going to go 150 years worth of data.

449
01:38:48,560 --> 01:38:53,000
And yeah, so 10 years ago, when the price of gas

450
01:38:53,000 --> 01:38:54,840
was really high historically, that's

451
01:38:54,840 --> 01:38:57,680
when I went and bet that it would fall a lot.

452
01:38:57,680 --> 01:38:59,160
And I won.

453
01:38:59,160 --> 01:39:01,080
So of course, Tyler will say, like, this

454
01:39:01,080 --> 01:39:02,040
doesn't prove anything.

455
01:39:02,040 --> 01:39:03,120
I just got lucky.

456
01:39:03,120 --> 01:39:03,620
Right?

457
01:39:03,620 --> 01:39:06,600
But I mean, that really doesn't prove anything.

458
01:39:06,600 --> 01:39:10,600
But that you would put no weight on it all seems kind of odd.

459
01:39:10,600 --> 01:39:11,100
Right?

460
01:39:11,100 --> 01:39:13,640
Again, especially when the actual,

461
01:39:13,640 --> 01:39:15,160
like the standard view among people

462
01:39:15,160 --> 01:39:18,080
know the data is gas is like petroleum in general

463
01:39:18,080 --> 01:39:20,720
is mean reversion.

464
01:39:20,720 --> 01:39:23,280
So again, as to why that might be,

465
01:39:23,280 --> 01:39:25,240
so again, like we're talking about commodities.

466
01:39:25,240 --> 01:39:28,480
The storage cost alone could give you mean reversion, right?

467
01:39:28,480 --> 01:39:32,960
So again, like suppose that the price of something

468
01:39:32,960 --> 01:39:35,440
is really high.

469
01:39:35,440 --> 01:39:35,960
Right?

470
01:39:35,960 --> 01:39:38,280
You might say, well, it's not possible it's going to go down,

471
01:39:38,280 --> 01:39:39,440
because people knew it was going to go down.

472
01:39:39,440 --> 01:39:41,160
And they would just sell off their whole stockpile.

473
01:39:41,160 --> 01:39:44,320
And that would drive prices back down to the normal level.

474
01:39:44,320 --> 01:39:44,820
All right?

475
01:39:44,820 --> 01:39:48,480
But again, what if it's actually a big problem

476
01:39:48,480 --> 01:39:50,600
to go and sell it off quickly?

477
01:39:50,600 --> 01:39:53,240
For example, like to sell off petroleum really quickly

478
01:39:53,240 --> 01:39:54,960
right now, suppose you knew that, like,

479
01:39:54,960 --> 01:39:56,400
suppose you owned an oil well.

480
01:39:56,400 --> 01:39:58,360
And you knew that next year the price of oil

481
01:39:58,360 --> 01:40:10,920
would fall by 90%?


1
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:04,300
Would you actually be able to pump every drop out this year?

2
01:40:04,300 --> 01:40:06,860
Probably pretty hard to pump every drop out this year.

3
01:40:06,860 --> 01:40:10,220
So that kind of thing alone can give you some mean reversion.

4
01:40:10,220 --> 01:40:13,680
If just the fact that the price is temporarily high,

5
01:40:13,680 --> 01:40:16,940
is not enough to induce perfect arbitrage.

6
01:40:16,940 --> 01:40:18,940
So, and then similarly,

7
01:40:18,940 --> 01:40:20,020
it could be that it's hard,

8
01:40:20,020 --> 01:40:22,100
so could be that you know that in 10 years,

